          <div class=3D"sect1" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
            <div class=3D"package" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Introduction to libcap with PAM
              </h3>
              <p>
                The <span class=3D"application">libcap</span> package was
                installed in LFS, but if <span class=3D"application">Linux-=
PAM</span> support is desired, the PAM
                module must be built (after installation of <span class=3D"=
application">Linux-PAM</span>).
              </p>
              <p>
                This package is known to build and work properly using an L=
FS
                12.2 platform.
              </p>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470535245088" name=3D"idm140470535245088"></=
a>Package Information
              </h4>
              <div class=3D"itemizedlist">
                <ul class=3D"compact">
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download (HTTP): <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://w=
ww.kernel.org/pub/linux/libs/security/linux-privs/libcap2/libcap-2.70.tar.x=
z">
                      https://www.kernel.org/pub/linux/libs/security/linux-=
privs/libcap2/libcap-2.70.tar.xz</a>
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download MD5 sum: df0e20c6eeca849347b87d5d6a8870c0
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download size: 188 KB
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Estimated disk space required: 2.2 MB
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Estimated build time: less than 0.1 SBU
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470535237648" name=3D"idm140470535237648"></=
a>libcap Dependencies
              </h4>
              <h5>
                <a id=3D"idm140470535236960" name=3D"idm140470535236960"></=
a>Required
              </h5>
              <p class=3D"required">
                <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/=
blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#linux-pam" title=3D=
"Linux-PAM-1.6.1">Linux-PAM-1.6.1</a>
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Installation of libcap
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  If you are upgrading libcap from a previous version, use
                  the instructions in <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://ww=
w.linuxfromscratch.org/lfs/view/12.2/chapter08/libcap.html">LFS
                  libcap page</a> to upgrade libcap. If <a class=3D"xref" h=
ref=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.=
2-sysv-nochunks.html#linux-pam" title=3D"Linux-PAM-1.6.1">Linux-PAM-1.6.1</=
a>
                  has been built, the PAM module will automatically be buil=
t
                  too.
                </p>
              </div>
              <p>
                Install <span class=3D"application">libcap</span> by runnin=
g
                the following commands:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">make -C pam_c=
ap</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                This package does not come with a test suite.
              </p>
              <p>
                Now, as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">install -v -m755 p=
am_cap/pam_cap.so /usr/lib/security &amp;&amp;
install -v -m644 pam_cap/capability.conf /etc/security</kbd></pre>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"configuration" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Configuring Libcap
              </h3>
              <p>
                In order to allow <span class=3D"application">Linux-PAM</sp=
an>
                to grant privileges based on POSIX capabilities, you need t=
o
                add the libcap module to the beginning of the <code class=
=3D"filename">/etc/pam.d/system-auth</code> file. Make the
                required edits with the following commands:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">mv -v /etc/pam.d/s=
ystem-auth{,.bak} &amp;&amp;
cat &gt; /etc/pam.d/system-auth &lt;&lt; "EOF" &amp;&amp;
<code class=3D"literal"># Begin /etc/pam.d/system-auth

auth      optional    pam_cap.so</code>
EOF
tail -n +3 /etc/pam.d/system-auth.bak &gt;&gt; /etc/pam.d/system-auth</kbd>=
</pre>
              <p>
                Additionally, you'll need to modify the <code class=3D"file=
name">/etc/security/capability.conf</code> file to grant
                necessary privileges to users, and utilize the <span class=
=3D"command"><strong>setcap</strong></span> utility to set
                capabilities on specific utilities as needed. See
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>man 8 setcap</strong></span=
>
                and <span class=3D"command"><strong>man 3
                cap_from_text</strong></span> for additional information.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"content" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
