          <div class=3D"titlepage">
            <div>
              <div>
                <h2 class=3D"title">
                  <a id=3D"mitkrb" name=3D"mitkrb"></a>MIT Kerberos V5-1.21=
.3
                </h2>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class=3D"sect1" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
            <div class=3D"package" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Introduction to MIT Kerberos V5
              </h3>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"application">MIT Kerberos V5</span> is a fre=
e
                implementation of Kerberos 5. Kerberos is a network
                authentication protocol. It centralizes the authentication
                database and uses kerberized applications to work with
                servers or services that support Kerberos allowing single
                logins and encrypted communication over internal networks o=
r
                the Internet.
              </p>
              <p>
                This package is known to build and work properly using an L=
FS
                12.2 platform.
              </p>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470534939440" name=3D"idm140470534939440"></=
a>Package Information
              </h4>
              <div class=3D"itemizedlist">
                <ul class=3D"compact">
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download (HTTP): <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://k=
erberos.org/dist/krb5/1.21/krb5-1.21.3.tar.gz">
                      https://kerberos.org/dist/krb5/1.21/krb5-1.21.3.tar.g=
z</a>
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download MD5 sum: beb34d1dfc72ba0571ce72bed03e06eb
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download size: 8.7 MB
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Estimated disk space required: 95 MB (add 14 MB for
                      tests)
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Estimated build time: 0.3 SBU (Using parallelism=3D4;=
 add
                      1.1 SBU for tests)
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470534930672" name=3D"idm140470534930672"></=
a>MIT
                Kerberos V5 Dependencies
              </h4>
              <h5>
                <a id=3D"idm140470534929968" name=3D"idm140470534929968"></=
a>Optional
              </h5>
              <p class=3D"optional">
                <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/=
blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#bind-utils" title=
=3D"BIND Utilities-9.20.0">BIND Utilities-9.20.0</a>, <a class=3D"xref" hre=
f=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-=
sysv-nochunks.html#cracklib" title=3D"CrackLib-2.10.2">CrackLib-2.10.2</a> =
(<code class=3D"filename">/usr/share/dict/words</code> referred by some
                tests), <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscra=
tch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#gnupg2" tit=
le=3D"GnuPG-2.4.5">GnuPG-2.4.5</a> (to authenticate the package),
                <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/=
blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#keyutils" title=3D"=
keyutils-1.6.3">keyutils-1.6.3</a>, <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.l=
inuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html=
#openldap" title=3D"OpenLDAP-2.6.8">OpenLDAP-2.6.8</a>,
                <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/=
blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#valgrind" title=3D"=
Valgrind-3.23.0">Valgrind-3.23.0</a> (used during the test
                suite), <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscra=
tch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#yasm" title=
=3D"yasm-1.3.0">yasm-1.3.0</a>, <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://thrysoee=
.dk/editline/">libedit</a>, <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://cmocka.org/"=
>cmocka</a>, <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://pypi.org/project/kdcproxy/"=
>kdcproxy</a>, <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://pypi.org/project/pyrad/">=
pyrad</a>, and
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://cwrap.org/resolv_wrapper=
.html">resolv_wrapper</a>
              </p>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  Some sort of time synchronization facility on your system
                  (like <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscra=
tch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#ntp" title=
=3D"ntp-4.2.8p18">ntp-4.2.8p18</a>) is required since Kerberos
                  won't authenticate if there is a time difference between =
a
                  kerberized client and the KDC server.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Installation of MIT Kerberos V5
              </h3>
              <p>
                Build <span class=3D"application">MIT Kerberos V5</span> by
                running the following commands:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">cd src &amp;&=
amp;
sed -i -e '/eq 0/{N;s/12 //}' plugins/kdb/db2/libdb2/test/run.test &amp;&am=
p;

./configure --prefix=3D/usr            \
            --sysconfdir=3D/etc        \
            --localstatedir=3D/var/lib \
            --runstatedir=3D/run       \
            --with-system-et         \
            --with-system-ss         \
            --with-system-verto=3Dno   \
            --enable-dns-for-realm   \
            --disable-rpath          &amp;&amp;
make</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                To test the build, issue: <span class=3D"command"><strong>m=
ake
                -j1 -k check</strong></span>. Some tests may fail with the
                latest version of dejagnu and glibc. Some tests may hang fo=
r
                a long time and fail if the system is not connected to a
                network. One test, <code class=3D"filename">t_kadm5srv</cod=
e>,
                is known to fail. If <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.=
linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.htm=
l#keyutils" title=3D"keyutils-1.6.3">keyutils-1.6.3</a> is installed but
                <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/=
blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#keyutils-test-kerne=
l" title=3D"Kernel Configuration">Keyutils Kernel Configuration</a> is
                not satisfied, some tests will fail complaining <code class=
=3D"computeroutput">keyctl failed with code 1</code>.
              </p>
              <p>
                Now, as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make install &amp;=
&amp;
cp -vfr ../doc -T /usr/share/doc/krb5-1.21.3</kbd></pre>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"commands" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Command Explanations
              </h3>
              <p>
                The <span class=3D"command"><strong>sed</strong></span> com=
mand
                removes a test that is known to fail.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--localstatedir=3D/var/lib</c=
ode></em>: This
                option is used so that the Kerberos variable runtime data i=
s
                located in <code class=3D"filename">/var/lib</code> instead=
 of
                <code class=3D"filename">/usr/var</code>.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--runstatedir=3D/run</code></=
em>:
                This option is used so that the Kerberos runtime state
                information is located in <code class=3D"filename">/run</co=
de>
                instead of the deprecated <code class=3D"filename">/var/run=
</code>.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-system-et</code></em>:
                This switch causes the build to use the system-installed
                versions of the error-table support software.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-system-ss</code></em>:
                This switch causes the build to use the system-installed
                versions of the subsystem command-line interface software.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-system-verto=3Dno</cod=
e></em>: This
                switch fixes a bug in the package: it does not recognize it=
s
                own verto library installed previously. This is not a
                problem, if reinstalling the same version, but if you are
                updating, the old library is used as system's one, instead =
of
                installing the new version.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--enable-dns-for-realm</code>=
</em>: This
                switch allows realms to be resolved using the DNS server.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--disable-rpath</code></em>: =
This
                switch prevents hard coding library search paths (rpath) in=
to
                the binary executable files and shared libraries. This
                package does not need rpath for an installation into the
                standard location, and rpath may sometimes cause unwanted
                effects or even security issues.
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">--with-ldap</code>: Use this switch =
if
                you want to compile the <span class=3D"application">OpenLDA=
P</span> database backend module.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"configuration" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Configuring MIT Kerberos V5
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"krb5-config" name=3D"krb5-config"></a>Conf=
ig
                        Files
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  <code class=3D"filename">/etc/krb5.conf</code> and
                  <code class=3D"filename">/var/lib/krb5kdc/kdc.conf</code>
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"idm140470534877216" name=3D"idm14047053487=
7216"></a>Configuration Information
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class=3D"sect4">
                  <div class=3D"titlepage">
                    <div>
                      <div>
                        <h5 class=3D"title">
                          <a id=3D"idm140470534876528" name=3D"idm140470534=
876528"></a>Kerberos Configuration
                        </h5>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <div class=3D"admon tip">
                    <h3>
                      Tip
                    </h3>
                    <p>
                      You should consider installing some sort of password
                      checking dictionary so that you can configure the
                      installation to only accept strong passwords. A
                      suitable dictionary to use is shown in the <a class=
=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BL=
FS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#cracklib" title=3D"CrackLib-2.10.2">CrackLi=
b-2.10.2</a> instructions.
                      Note that only one file can be used, but you can
                      concatenate many files into one. The configuration fi=
le
                      shown below assumes you have installed a dictionary t=
o
                      <code class=3D"filename">/usr/share/dict/words</code>=
.
                    </p>
                  </div>
                  <p>
                    Create the Kerberos configuration file with the followi=
ng
                    commands issued by the <code class=3D"systemitem">root<=
/code> user:
                  </p>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt; /etc/=
krb5.conf &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal"># Begin /etc/krb5.conf

[libdefaults]
    default_realm =3D <em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;EXAMPLE.ORG&gt;</=
code></em>
    encrypt =3D true

[realms]
    <em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;EXAMPLE.ORG&gt;</code></em> =3D {
        kdc =3D <em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;belgarath.example.org&g=
t;</code></em>
        admin_server =3D <em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;belgarath.exam=
ple.org&gt;</code></em>
        dict_file =3D /usr/share/dict/words
    }

[domain_realm]
    .<em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;example.org&gt;</code></em> =3D <e=
m class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;EXAMPLE.ORG&gt;</code></em>

[logging]
    kdc =3D SYSLOG:INFO:AUTH
    admin_server =3D SYSLOG:INFO:AUTH
    default =3D SYSLOG:DEBUG:DAEMON

# End /etc/krb5.conf</code>
EOF</kbd></pre>
                  <p>
                    You will need to substitute your domain and proper
                    hostname for the occurrences of the <em class=3D"replac=
eable"><code>&lt;belgarath&gt;</code></em> and
                    <em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;example.org&gt;</co=
de></em>
                    names.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    <code class=3D"option">default_realm</code> should be t=
he
                    name of your domain changed to ALL CAPS. This isn't
                    required, but both <span class=3D"application">Heimdal<=
/span> and MIT recommend it.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    <code class=3D"option">encrypt =3D true</code> provides
                    encryption of all traffic between kerberized clients an=
d
                    servers. It's not necessary and can be left off. If you
                    leave it off, you can encrypt all traffic from the clie=
nt
                    to the server using a switch on the client program
                    instead.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    The <code class=3D"option">[realms]</code> parameters t=
ell
                    the client programs where to look for the KDC
                    authentication services.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    The <code class=3D"option">[domain_realm]</code> sectio=
n
                    maps a domain to a realm.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    Create the KDC database:
                  </p>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">kdb5_util crea=
te -r <em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;EXAMPLE.ORG&gt;</code></em> -s</k=
bd></pre>
                  <p>
                    Now you should populate the database with principals
                    (users). For now, just use your regular login name or
                    <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code>.
                  </p>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">kadmin.local
<code class=3D"prompt">kadmin.local:</code> add_policy dict-only
<code class=3D"prompt">kadmin.local:</code> addprinc -policy dict-only <em =
class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;loginname&gt;</code></em></kbd></pre>
                  <p>
                    The KDC server and any machine running kerberized serve=
r
                    daemons must have a host key installed:
                  </p>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command"><code class=3D=
"prompt">kadmin.local:</code> addprinc -randkey host/<em class=3D"replaceab=
le"><code>&lt;belgarath.example.org&gt;</code></em></kbd></pre>
                  <p>
                    After choosing the defaults when prompted, you will hav=
e
                    to export the data to a keytab file:
                  </p>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command"><code class=3D=
"prompt">kadmin.local:</code> ktadd host/<em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&l=
t;belgarath.example.org&gt;</code></em></kbd></pre>
                  <p>
                    This should have created a file in <code class=3D"filen=
ame">/etc</code> named <code class=3D"filename">krb5.keytab</code> (Kerbero=
s 5). This file
                    should have 600 (<code class=3D"systemitem">root</code>=
 rw
                    only) permissions. Keeping the keytab files from public
                    access is crucial to the overall security of the Kerber=
os
                    installation.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    Exit the <span class=3D"command"><strong>kadmin</strong=
></span> program (use
                    <span class=3D"command"><strong>quit</strong></span> or
                    <span class=3D"command"><strong>exit</strong></span>) a=
nd
                    return back to the shell prompt. Start the KDC daemon
                    manually, just to test out the installation:
                  </p>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">/usr/sbin/krb5=
kdc</kbd></pre>
                  <p>
                    Attempt to get a ticket with the following command:
                  </p>
                  <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">kinit <em=
 class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;loginname&gt;</code></em></kbd></pre>
                  <p>
                    You will be prompted for the password you created. Afte=
r
                    you get your ticket, you can list it with the following
                    command:
                  </p>
                  <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">klist</kb=
d></pre>
                  <p>
                    Information about the ticket should be displayed on the
                    screen.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    To test the functionality of the keytab file, issue the
                    following command as the <code class=3D"systemitem">roo=
t</code> user:
                  </p>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">ktutil
<code class=3D"prompt">ktutil:</code> rkt /etc/krb5.keytab
<code class=3D"prompt">ktutil:</code> l</kbd></pre>
                  <p>
                    This should dump a list of the host principal, along wi=
th
                    the encryption methods used to access the principal.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    Create an empty ACL file that can be modified later:
                  </p>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">touch /var/lib=
/krb5kdc/kadm5.acl</kbd></pre>
                  <p>
                    At this point, if everything has been successful so far=
,
                    you can feel fairly confident in the installation and
                    configuration of the package.
                  </p>
                </div>
