              <div>
                <h2 class=3D"title">
                  <a id=3D"shadow" name=3D"shadow"></a>Shadow-4.16.0
                </h2>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class=3D"sect1" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
            <div class=3D"package" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Introduction to Shadow
              </h3>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"application">Shadow</span> was indeed instal=
led
                in LFS and there is no reason to reinstall it unless you
                installed <span class=3D"application">CrackLib</span> or
                <span class=3D"application">Linux-PAM</span> after your LFS
                system was completed. If you have installed <span class=3D"=
application">CrackLib</span> after LFS, then reinstalling
                <span class=3D"application">Shadow</span> will enable stron=
g
                password support. If you have installed <span class=3D"appl=
ication">Linux-PAM</span>, reinstalling <span class=3D"application">Shadow<=
/span> will allow programs such as
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>login</strong></span> and
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>su</strong></span> to utili=
ze
                PAM.
              </p>
              <p>
                This package is known to build and work properly using an L=
FS
                12.2 platform.
              </p>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470534069712" name=3D"idm140470534069712"></=
a>Package Information
              </h4>
              <div class=3D"itemizedlist">
                <ul class=3D"compact">
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download (HTTP): <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://g=
ithub.com/shadow-maint/shadow/releases/download/4.16.0/shadow-4.16.0.tar.xz=
">
                      https://github.com/shadow-maint/shadow/releases/downl=
oad/4.16.0/shadow-4.16.0.tar.xz</a>
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download MD5 sum: eb70bad3316d08f0d3bb3d4bbeccb3b4
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download size: 2.1 MB
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Estimated disk space required: 100 MB
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Estimated build time: 0.2 SBU
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470534061296" name=3D"idm140470534061296"></=
a>Shadow Dependencies
              </h4>
              <h5>
                <a id=3D"idm140470534060608" name=3D"idm140470534060608"></=
a>Required
              </h5>
              <p class=3D"required">
                <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/=
blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#linux-pam" title=3D=
"Linux-PAM-1.6.1">Linux-PAM-1.6.1</a> or <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://=
www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks=
.html#cracklib" title=3D"CrackLib-2.10.2">CrackLib-2.10.2</a>
              </p>
              <h5>
                <a id=3D"idm140470534057680" name=3D"idm140470534057680"></=
a>Optional
              </h5>
              <p class=3D"optional">
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://libbsd.freedesktop.org/w=
iki/">libbsd</a> and
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://www.openwall.com/tcb/">t=
cb</a>
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Installation of Shadow
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"admon important">
                <h3>
                  Important
                </h3>
                <p>
                  The installation commands shown below are for installatio=
ns
                  where <span class=3D"application">Linux-PAM</span> has be=
en
                  installed and <span class=3D"application">Shadow</span> i=
s
                  being reinstalled to support the <span class=3D"applicati=
on">Linux-PAM</span> installation.
                </p>
                <p>
                  If you are reinstalling <span class=3D"application">Shado=
w</span> to provide strong password
                  support using the <span class=3D"application">CrackLib</s=
pan>
                  library without using <span class=3D"application">Linux-P=
AM</span>, ensure you add the
                  <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-libcrack</code></em>
                  parameter to the <span class=3D"command"><strong>configur=
e</strong></span> script below
                  and also issue the following command:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">sed -i 's@D=
ICTPATH.*@DICTPATH\t/lib/cracklib/pw_dict@' etc/login.defs</kbd></pre>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"admon warning">
                <h3>
                  Warning
                </h3>
                <p>
                  If reinstalling shadow for a version update, be sure to
                  reaccomplish the Linux-PAM configuration below. The
                  installation of shadow overwrites many of the files in
                  <code class=3D"filename">/etc/pam.d/</code>.
                </p>
              </div>
              <p>
                Reinstall <span class=3D"application">Shadow</span> by runn=
ing
                the following commands:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">sed -i 's/gro=
ups$(EXEEXT) //' src/Makefile.in          &amp;&amp;

find man -name Makefile.in -exec sed -i 's/groups\.1 / /'   {} \; &amp;&amp=
;
find man -name Makefile.in -exec sed -i 's/getspnam\.3 / /' {} \; &amp;&amp=
;
find man -name Makefile.in -exec sed -i 's/passwd\.5 / /'   {} \; &amp;&amp=
;

sed -e 's@#ENCRYPT_METHOD DES@ENCRYPT_METHOD YESCRYPT@' \
    -e 's@/var/spool/mail@/var/mail@'                   \
    -e '/PATH=3D/{s@/sbin:@@;s@/bin:@@}'                  \
    -i etc/login.defs                                   &amp;&amp;

./configure --sysconfdir=3D/etc   \
            --disable-static    \
            --without-libbsd    \
            --with-{b,yes}crypt &amp;&amp;
make</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                This package does not come with a test suite.
              </p>
              <p>
                Now, as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make exec_prefix=
=3D/usr pamddir=3D install</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                The man pages were installed in LFS, but if reinstallation =
is
                desired, run (as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code>
                user):
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make -C man instal=
l-man</kbd></pre>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"commands" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Command Explanations
              </h3>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>sed -i 's/groups$(EXEEXT) /=
/'
                src/Makefile.in</strong></span>: This sed is used to suppre=
ss
                the installation of the <span class=3D"command"><strong>gro=
ups</strong></span> program as the
                version from the <span class=3D"application">Coreutils</spa=
n>
                package installed during LFS is preferred.
              </p>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>find man -name Makefile.in
                -exec ... {} \;</strong></span>: The first command is used =
to
                suppress the installation of the <span class=3D"command"><s=
trong>groups</strong></span> man pages so the
                existing ones installed from the <span class=3D"application=
">Coreutils</span> package are not replaced. The
                two other commands prevent installation of manual pages tha=
t
                are already installed by <span class=3D"application">Man-pa=
ges</span> in LFS.
              </p>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>sed -e 's@#ENCRYPT_METHOD
                DES@ENCRYPT_METHOD YESCRYPT@' -e
                's@/var/spool/mail@/var/mail@' -e
                '/PATH=3D/{s@/sbin:@@;s@/bin:@@}' -i
                etc/login.defs</strong></span>: Instead of using the defaul=
t
                'DES' method, this command modifies the installation to use
                the much more secure 'YESCRYPT' method of hashing passwords=
,
                which also allows passwords longer than eight characters. T=
he
                command also changes the obsolete <code class=3D"filename">=
/var/spool/mail</code> location for user mailboxes
                that <span class=3D"application">Shadow</span> uses by defa=
ult
                to the <code class=3D"filename">/var/mail</code> location. =
It
                also changes the default path to be consistent with that se=
t
                in LFS.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--without-libbsd</code></em>:
                Prevents looking for the <span class=3D"command"><strong>re=
adpassphrase</strong></span> function,
                which can be found only in <code class=3D"filename">libbsd<=
/code>, which we do not have in BLFS. An
                internal implementation of <span class=3D"command"><strong>=
readpassphrase</strong></span> is used
                instead.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>pamddir=3D</code></em>: Preve=
nts
                installation of the shipped PAM configuration files into
                <code class=3D"filename">/etc/pam.d</code>. The shipped
                configuration does not work with the BLFS PAM configuration
                and we will create these configuration files explicitly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"configuration" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Configuring Linux-PAM to Work with Shadow
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  The rest of this page is devoted to configuring
                  <span class=3D"application">Shadow</span> to work properl=
y
                  with <span class=3D"application">Linux-PAM</span>. If you=
 do
                  not have <span class=3D"application">Linux-PAM</span>
                  installed, and you reinstalled <span class=3D"application=
">Shadow</span> to support strong passwords via
                  the <span class=3D"application">CrackLib</span> library, =
no
                  further configuration is required.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"pam.d" name=3D"pam.d"></a>Config Files
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  <code class=3D"filename">/etc/pam.d/*</code> or alternati=
vely
                  <code class=3D"filename">/etc/pam.conf</code>, <code clas=
s=3D"filename">/etc/login.defs</code> and <code class=3D"filename">/etc/sec=
urity/*</code>
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"idm140470533994080" name=3D"idm14047053399=
4080"></a>Configuration Information
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  Configuring your system to use <span class=3D"application=
">Linux-PAM</span> can be a complex task. The
                  information below will provide a basic setup so that
                  <span class=3D"application">Shadow</span>'s login and
                  password functionality will work effectively with
                  <span class=3D"application">Linux-PAM</span>. Review the
                  information and links on the <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"ht=
tps://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-no=
chunks.html#linux-pam" title=3D"Linux-PAM-1.6.1">Linux-PAM-1.6.1</a>
                  page for further configuration information. For informati=
on
                  specific to integrating <span class=3D"application">Shado=
w</span>, <span class=3D"application">Linux-PAM</span> and <span class=3D"a=
pplication">libpwquality</span>, you can visit the
                  following link:
                </p>
                <div class=3D"itemizedlist">
                  <ul class=3D"compact">
                    <li class=3D"listitem">
                      <p>
                        <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://deer-run.com/use=
rs/hal/linux_passwords_pam.html">
                        https://deer-run.com/users/hal/linux_passwords_pam.=
html</a>
                      </p>
                    </li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <div class=3D"sect4">
                  <div class=3D"titlepage">
                    <div>
                      <div>
                        <h5 class=3D"title">
                          <a id=3D"pam-login-defs" name=3D"pam-login-defs">=
</a>Configuring /etc/login.defs
                        </h5>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <p>
                    The <span class=3D"command"><strong>login</strong></spa=
n>
                    program currently performs many functions which
                    <span class=3D"application">Linux-PAM</span> modules sh=
ould
                    now handle. The following <span class=3D"command"><stro=
ng>sed</strong></span> command will
                    comment out the appropriate lines in <code class=3D"fil=
ename">/etc/login.defs</code>, and stop <span class=3D"command"><strong>log=
in</strong></span> from performing
                    these functions (a backup file named <code class=3D"fil=
ename">/etc/login.defs.orig</code> is also created to
                    preserve the original file's contents). Issue the
                    following commands as the <code class=3D"systemitem">ro=
ot</code> user:
                  </p>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">install -v -m6=
44 /etc/login.defs /etc/login.defs.orig &amp;&amp;
for FUNCTION in FAIL_DELAY               \
                FAILLOG_ENAB             \
                LASTLOG_ENAB             \
                MAIL_CHECK_ENAB          \
                OBSCURE_CHECKS_ENAB      \
                PORTTIME_CHECKS_ENAB     \
                QUOTAS_ENAB              \
                CONSOLE MOTD_FILE        \
                FTMP_FILE NOLOGINS_FILE  \
                ENV_HZ PASS_MIN_LEN      \
                SU_WHEEL_ONLY            \
                CRACKLIB_DICTPATH        \
                PASS_CHANGE_TRIES        \
                PASS_ALWAYS_WARN         \
                CHFN_AUTH ENCRYPT_METHOD \
                ENVIRON_FILE
do
    sed -i "s/^${FUNCTION}/# &amp;/" /etc/login.defs
done</kbd></pre>
                </div>
                <div class=3D"sect4">
                  <div class=3D"titlepage">
                    <div>
                      <div>
                        <h5 class=3D"title">
                          <a id=3D"idm140470533973328" name=3D"idm140470533=
973328"></a>Configuring the
                          /etc/pam.d/ Files
                        </h5>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <p>
                    As mentioned previously in the <span class=3D"applicati=
on">Linux-PAM</span> instructions, <span class=3D"application">Linux-PAM</s=
pan> has two supported methods
                    for configuration. The commands below assume that you'v=
e
                    chosen to use a directory based configuration, where ea=
ch
                    program has its own configuration file. You can
                    optionally use a single <code class=3D"filename">/etc/p=
am.conf</code> configuration file by
                    using the text from the files below, and supplying the
                    program name as an additional first field for each line=
.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    As the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user, cre=
ate
                    the following <span class=3D"application">Linux-PAM</sp=
an>
                    configuration files in the <code class=3D"filename">/et=
c/pam.d/</code> directory (or add the
                    contents to the <code class=3D"filename">/etc/pam.conf<=
/code> file) using the following
                    commands:
                  </p>
                </div>
                <div class=3D"sect4">
                  <div class=3D"titlepage">
                    <div>
                      <div>
                        <h5 class=3D"title">
                          <a id=3D"idm140470533965568" name=3D"idm140470533=
965568"></a>'login'
                        </h5>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt; /etc/=
pam.d/login &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal"># Begin /etc/pam.d/login

# Set failure delay before next prompt to 3 seconds
auth      optional    pam_faildelay.so  delay=3D3000000

# Check to make sure that the user is allowed to login
auth      requisite   pam_nologin.so

# Check to make sure that root is allowed to login
# Disabled by default. You will need to create /etc/securetty
# file for this module to function. See man 5 securetty.
#auth      required    pam_securetty.so

# Additional group memberships - disabled by default
#auth      optional    pam_group.so

# include system auth settings
auth      include     system-auth

# check access for the user
account   required    pam_access.so

# include system account settings
account   include     system-account

# Set default environment variables for the user
session   required    pam_env.so

# Set resource limits for the user
session   required    pam_limits.so

# Display the message of the day - Disabled by default
#session   optional    pam_motd.so

# Check user's mail - Disabled by default
#session   optional    pam_mail.so      standard quiet

# include system session and password settings
session   include     system-session
password  include     system-password

# End /etc/pam.d/login</code>
EOF</kbd></pre>
                </div>
                <div class=3D"sect4">
                  <div class=3D"titlepage">
                    <div>
                      <div>
                        <h5 class=3D"title">
                          <a id=3D"idm140470533961440" name=3D"idm140470533=
961440"></a>'passwd'
                        </h5>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt; /etc/=
pam.d/passwd &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal"># Begin /etc/pam.d/passwd

password  include     system-password

# End /etc/pam.d/passwd</code>
EOF</kbd></pre>
                </div>
                <div class=3D"sect4">
                  <div class=3D"titlepage">
                    <div>
                      <div>
                        <h5 class=3D"title">
                          <a id=3D"idm140470533958432" name=3D"idm140470533=
958432"></a>'su'
                        </h5>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt; /etc/=
pam.d/su &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal"># Begin /etc/pam.d/su

# always allow root
auth      sufficient  pam_rootok.so

# Allow users in the wheel group to execute su without a password
# disabled by default
#auth      sufficient  pam_wheel.so trust use_uid

# include system auth settings
auth      include     system-auth

# limit su to users in the wheel group
# disabled by default
#auth      required    pam_wheel.so use_uid

# include system account settings
account   include     system-account

# Set default environment variables for the service user
session   required    pam_env.so

# include system session settings
session   include     system-session

# End /etc/pam.d/su</code>
EOF</kbd></pre>
                </div>
                <div class=3D"sect4">
                  <div class=3D"titlepage">
                    <div>
                      <div>
                        <h5 class=3D"title">
                          <a id=3D"idm140470533955552" name=3D"idm140470533=
955552"></a>'chpasswd' and 'newusers'
                        </h5>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt; /etc/=
pam.d/chpasswd &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal"># Begin /etc/pam.d/chpasswd

# always allow root
auth      sufficient  pam_rootok.so

# include system auth and account settings
auth      include     system-auth
account   include     system-account
password  include     system-password

# End /etc/pam.d/chpasswd</code>
EOF

sed -e s/chpasswd/newusers/ /etc/pam.d/chpasswd &gt;/etc/pam.d/newusers</kb=
d></pre>
                </div>
                <div class=3D"sect4">
                  <div class=3D"titlepage">
                    <div>
                      <div>
                        <h5 class=3D"title">
                          <a id=3D"idm140470533952256" name=3D"idm140470533=
952256"></a>'chage'
                        </h5>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt; /etc/=
pam.d/chage &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal"># Begin /etc/pam.d/chage

# always allow root
auth      sufficient  pam_rootok.so

# include system auth and account settings
auth      include     system-auth
account   include     system-account

# End /etc/pam.d/chage</code>
EOF</kbd></pre>
                </div>
                <div class=3D"sect4">
                  <div class=3D"titlepage">
                    <div>
                      <div>
                        <h5 class=3D"title">
                          <a id=3D"idm140470533949120" name=3D"idm140470533=
949120"></a>Other shadow utilities
                        </h5>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">for PROGRAM in=
 chfn chgpasswd chsh groupadd groupdel \
               groupmems groupmod useradd userdel usermod
do
    install -v -m644 /etc/pam.d/chage /etc/pam.d/${PROGRAM}
    sed -i "s/chage/$PROGRAM/" /etc/pam.d/${PROGRAM}
done</kbd></pre>
                  <div class=3D"admon warning">
                    <h3>
                      Warning
                    </h3>
                    <p>
                      At this point, you should do a simple test to see if
                      <span class=3D"application">Shadow</span> is working =
as
                      expected. Open another terminal and log in as
                      <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code>, and then run
                      <span class=3D"command"><strong>login</strong></span>=
 and
                      login as another user. If you do not see any errors,
                      then all is well and you should proceed with the rest
                      of the configuration. If you did receive errors, stop
                      now and double check the above configuration files
                      manually. Any error is the sign of an error in the
                      above procedure. You can also run the test suite from
                      the <span class=3D"application">Linux-PAM</span> pack=
age
                      to assist you in determining the problem. If you cann=
ot
                      find and fix the error, you should recompile
                      <span class=3D"application">Shadow</span> adding the
                      <code class=3D"option">--without-libpam</code> switch=
 to
                      the <span class=3D"command"><strong>configure</strong=
></span> command in
                      the above instructions (also move the <code class=3D"=
filename">/etc/login.defs.orig</code> backup file to
                      <code class=3D"filename">/etc/login.defs</code>). If =
you
                      fail to do this and the errors remain, you will be
                      unable to log into your system.
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class=3D"sect4">
                  <div class=3D"titlepage">
                    <div>
                      <div>
                        <h5 class=3D"title">
                          <a id=3D"pam-access" name=3D"pam-access"></a>Conf=
iguring Login Access
                        </h5>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <p>
                    Instead of using the <code class=3D"filename">/etc/logi=
n.access</code> file for controlling
                    access to the system, <span class=3D"application">Linux=
-PAM</span> uses the <code class=3D"filename">pam_access.so</code> module a=
long with the
                    <code class=3D"filename">/etc/security/access.conf</cod=
e>
                    file. Rename the <code class=3D"filename">/etc/login.ac=
cess</code> file using the
                    following command:
                  </p>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">if [ -f /etc/l=
ogin.access ]; then mv -v /etc/login.access{,.NOUSE}; fi</kbd></pre>
                </div>
                <div class=3D"sect4">
                  <div class=3D"titlepage">
                    <div>
                      <div>
                        <h5 class=3D"title">
                          <a id=3D"pam-limits" name=3D"pam-limits"></a>Conf=
iguring Resource Limits
                        </h5>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <p>
                    Instead of using the <code class=3D"filename">/etc/limi=
ts</code> file for limiting usage of
                    system resources, <span class=3D"application">Linux-PAM=
</span> uses the <code class=3D"filename">pam_limits.so</code> module along=
 with the
                    <code class=3D"filename">/etc/security/limits.conf</cod=
e>
                    file. Rename the <code class=3D"filename">/etc/limits</=
code> file using the following
                    command:
                  </p>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">if [ -f /etc/l=
imits ]; then mv -v /etc/limits{,.NOUSE}; fi</kbd></pre>
                  <div class=3D"admon caution">
                    <h3>
                      Caution
                    </h3>
                    <p>
                      Be sure to test the login capabilities of the system
                      before logging out. Errors in the configuration can
                      cause a permanent lockout requiring a boot from an
                      external source to correct the problem.
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"content" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Contents
              </h3>
              <p>
                A list of the installed files, along with their short
                descriptions can be found at <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"htt=
ps://www.linuxfromscratch.org/lfs/view/12.2/chapter08/shadow.html#contents-=
shadow">
                ../../../../lfs/view/12.2/chapter08/shadow.html#contents-sh=
adow</a>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class=3D"titlepage">
            <div>
              <div>
                <h2 class=3D"title">
                  <a id=3D"ssh-askpass" name=3D"ssh-askpass"></a>ssh-askpas=
s-9.8p1
                </h2>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class=3D"sect1" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
            <div class=3D"package" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Introduction to ssh-askpass
              </h3>
              <p>
                The <span class=3D"application">ssh-askpass</span> is a gen=
eric
                executable name for many packages, with similar names, that
                provide a interactive X service to grab password for packag=
es
                requiring administrative privileges to be run. It prompts t=
he
                user with a window box where the necessary password can be
                inserted. Here, we choose Damien Miller's package distribut=
ed
                in the <span class=3D"application">OpenSSH</span> tarball.
              </p>
              <p>
                This package is known to build and work properly using an L=
FS
                12.2 platform.
              </p>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470533909952" name=3D"idm140470533909952"></=
a>Package Information
              </h4>
              <div class=3D"itemizedlist">
                <ul class=3D"compact">
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download (HTTP): <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://f=
tp.openbsd.org/pub/OpenBSD/OpenSSH/portable/openssh-9.8p1.tar.gz">
                      https://ftp.openbsd.org/pub/OpenBSD/OpenSSH/portable/=
openssh-9.8p1.tar.gz</a>
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download MD5 sum: bc04ff77796758c0b37bd0bc9314cd3f
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download size: 1.8 MB
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Estimated disk space required: 10 MB
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Estimated build time: less than 0.1 SBU
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470533902512" name=3D"idm140470533902512"></=
a>ssh-askpass Dependencies
              </h4>
              <h5>
                <a id=3D"idm140470533901808" name=3D"idm140470533901808"></=
a>Required
              </h5>
              <p class=3D"required">
                <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/=
blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#gtk3" title=3D"GTK+=
-3.24.43">GTK+-3.24.43</a>, <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfrom=
scratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#sudo" t=
itle=3D"Sudo-1.9.15p5">Sudo-1.9.15p5</a> (runtime), <a class=3D"xref" href=
=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-s=
ysv-nochunks.html#xorg7-lib" title=3D"Xorg Libraries">Xorg
                Libraries</a>, and <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.li=
nuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#=
x-window-system" title=3D"Chapter&nbsp;24.&nbsp;Graphical Environments">a
                graphical environment</a> (runtime)
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Installation of ssh-askpass
              </h3>
              <p>
                Install <span class=3D"application">ssh-askpass</span> by
                running the following commands:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">cd contrib &a=
mp;&amp;
make gnome-ssh-askpass3</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                Now, as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">install -v -d -m75=
5                    /usr/libexec/openssh/contrib  &amp;&amp;
install -v -m755    gnome-ssh-askpass3 /usr/libexec/openssh/contrib  &amp;&=
amp;
ln -sv -f contrib/gnome-ssh-askpass3   /usr/libexec/openssh/ssh-askpass</kb=
d></pre>
              <p>
                The use of /usr/libexec/openssh/contrib and a symlink is
                justified by the eventual necessity of a different program
                for that service.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"configuration" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Configuring ssh-askpass
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"ssh-askpass-config" name=3D"ssh-askpass-co=
nfig"></a>Configuration Information
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  As the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user, confi=
gure
                  <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.or=
g/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#sudo" title=3D"Su=
do-1.9.15p5">Sudo-1.9.15p5</a> to use <span class=3D"application">ssh-askpa=
ss</span>:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt;&gt; /et=
c/sudo.conf &lt;&lt; "EOF" &amp;&amp;
<code class=3D"literal"># Path to askpass helper program
Path askpass /usr/libexec/openssh/ssh-askpass</code>
EOF
chmod -v 0644 /etc/sudo.conf</kbd></pre>
                <p>
                  If a given graphical &lt;application&gt; requires
                  administrative privileges, use <span class=3D"command"><s=
trong>sudo -A
                  &lt;application&gt;</strong></span> from an x-terminal,
                  from a Window Manager menu and/or replace
                  "Exec=3D&lt;application&gt; ..." by "Exec=3Dsudo -A
                  &lt;application&gt; ..." in the &lt;application&gt;.deskt=
op
                  file.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"content" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Contents
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"segmentedlist">
                <div class=3D"seglistitem">
                  <div class=3D"seg">
                    <strong class=3D"segtitle">Installed Programs:</strong>
                    <span class=3D"segbody">gnome-ssh-askpass3, ssh-askpass
                    (symlink to <code class=3D"filename">gnome-ssh-askpass3=
</code>)</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class=3D"seg">
                    <strong class=3D"segtitle">Installed Library:</strong>
                    <span class=3D"segbody">None</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class=3D"seg">
                    <strong class=3D"segtitle">Installed Directory:</strong=
>
                    <span class=3D"segbody">/usr/libexec/openssh/contrib</s=
pan>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class=3D"titlepage">
            <div>
              <div>
                <h2 class=3D"title">
                  <a id=3D"stunnel" name=3D"stunnel"></a>stunnel-5.72
                </h2>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class=3D"sect1" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
            <div class=3D"package" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Introduction to stunnel
              </h3>
              <p>
                The <span class=3D"application">stunnel</span> package cont=
ains
                a program that allows you to encrypt arbitrary TCP
                connections inside SSL (Secure Sockets Layer) so you can
                easily communicate with clients over secure channels.
                <span class=3D"application">stunnel</span> can also be used=
 to
                tunnel PPP over network sockets without changes to the serv=
er
                package source code.
              </p>
              <p>
                This package is known to build and work properly using an L=
FS
                12.2 platform.
              </p>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470533867776" name=3D"idm140470533867776"></=
a>Package Information
              </h4>
              <div class=3D"itemizedlist">
                <ul class=3D"compact">
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download (HTTP): <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://w=
ww.stunnel.org/downloads/archive/5.x/stunnel-5.72.tar.gz">
                      https://www.stunnel.org/downloads/archive/5.x/stunnel=
-5.72.tar.gz</a>
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download MD5 sum: 61d35d2df1b96b23ed6fb20cf5ddb613
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download size: 880 KB
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Estimated disk space required: 7.5 MB
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Estimated build time: less than 0.1 SBU
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470533860352" name=3D"idm140470533860352"></=
a>stunnel Dependencies
              </h4>
              <h5>
                <a id=3D"idm140470533859664" name=3D"idm140470533859664"></=
a>Optional
              </h5>
              <p class=3D"optional">
                <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/=
blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#libnsl" title=3D"li=
bnsl-2.0.1">libnsl-2.0.1</a>, <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://netcat.sou=
rceforge.net/">netcat</a> (required for
                tests), <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"http://ftp.porcupine.org=
/pub/security/index.html">tcpwrappers</a>,
                and <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://dist.torproject.org/=
">TOR</a>
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Installation of stunnel
              </h3>
              <p>
                The <span class=3D"command"><strong>stunnel</strong></span>
                daemon will be run in a <span class=3D"command"><strong>chr=
oot</strong></span> jail by an
                unprivileged user. Create the new user and group using the
                following commands as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</=
code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">groupadd -g 51 stu=
nnel &amp;&amp;
useradd -c "stunnel Daemon" -d /var/lib/stunnel \
        -g stunnel -s /bin/false -u 51 stunnel</kbd></pre>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  A signed SSL Certificate and a Private Key is necessary t=
o
                  run the <span class=3D"command"><strong>stunnel</strong><=
/span> daemon. After the
                  package is installed, there are instructions to generate
                  them. However, if you own or have already created a signe=
d
                  SSL Certificate you wish to use, copy it to <code class=
=3D"filename">/etc/stunnel/stunnel.pem</code> before starting
                  the build (ensure only <code class=3D"systemitem">root</c=
ode>
                  has read and write access). The <code class=3D"filename">=
.pem</code> file must be formatted as shown
                  below:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"screen"><code class=3D"literal">-----BEGIN PR=
IVATE KEY-----
<em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;many encrypted lines of private key&gt;=
</code></em>
-----END PRIVATE KEY-----
-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----
<em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;many encrypted lines of certificate&gt;=
</code></em>
-----END CERTIFICATE-----
-----BEGIN DH PARAMETERS-----
<em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;encrypted lines of dh parms&gt;</code><=
/em>
-----END DH PARAMETERS-----</code></pre>
              </div>
              <p>
                Install <span class=3D"application">stunnel</span> by runni=
ng
                the following commands:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">./configure -=
-prefix=3D/usr        \
            --sysconfdir=3D/etc    \
            --localstatedir=3D/var \
            --disable-systemd    &amp;&amp;
make</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                If you have installed the optional netcat application, the
                regression tests can be run with <span class=3D"command"><s=
trong>make check</strong></span>.
              </p>
              <p>
                Now, as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make docdir=3D/usr=
/share/doc/stunnel-5.72 install</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                If you do not already have a signed SSL Certificate and
                Private Key, create the <code class=3D"filename">stunnel.pe=
m</code> file in the <code class=3D"filename">/etc/stunnel</code> directory=
 using the command
                below. You will be prompted to enter the necessary
                information. Ensure you reply to the
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"screen"><code class=3D"prompt">Common Name (FQD=
N of your server) [localhost]:</code></pre>
              <p>
                prompt with the name or IP address you will be using to
                access the service(s).
              </p>
              <p>
                To generate a certificate, as the <code class=3D"systemitem=
">root</code> user, issue:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make cert</kbd></p=
re>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"commands" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Command Explanations
              </h3>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--disable-systemd</code></em>=
:
                This switch disables systemd socket activation support whic=
h
                is not available in BLFS.
              </p>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>make docdir=3D...
                install</strong></span>: This command installs the package
                and changes the documentation installation directory to
                standard naming conventions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"configuration" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Configuring stunnel
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"stunnel-config" name=3D"stunnel-config"></=
a>Config Files
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  <code class=3D"filename">/etc/stunnel/stunnel.conf</code>
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"idm140470533819808" name=3D"idm14047053381=
9808"></a>Configuration Information
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  As the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user, creat=
e
                  the directory used for the <code class=3D"filename">.pid<=
/code> file created when the <span class=3D"application">stunnel</span> dae=
mon starts:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">install -v -m750=
 -o stunnel -g stunnel -d /var/lib/stunnel/run &amp;&amp;
chown stunnel:stunnel /var/lib/stunnel</kbd></pre>
                <p>
                  Next, create a basic <code class=3D"filename">/etc/stunne=
l/stunnel.conf</code> configuration
                  file using the following commands as the <code class=3D"s=
ystemitem">root</code> user:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt; /etc/st=
unnel/stunnel.conf &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal">; File: /etc/stunnel/stunnel.conf

; Note: The pid and output locations are relative to the chroot location.

pid    =3D /run/stunnel.pid
chroot =3D /var/lib/stunnel
client =3D no
setuid =3D stunnel
setgid =3D stunnel
cert   =3D /etc/stunnel/stunnel.pem

;debug =3D 7
;output =3D stunnel.log

;[https]
;accept  =3D 443
;connect =3D 80
;; "TIMEOUTclose =3D 0" is a workaround for a design flaw in Microsoft SSL
;; Microsoft implementations do not use SSL close-notify alert and thus
;; they are vulnerable to truncation attacks
;TIMEOUTclose =3D 0</code>

EOF</kbd></pre>
                <p>
                  Finally, add the service(s) you wish to encrypt to the
                  configuration file. The format is as follows:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"screen"><code class=3D"literal">[<em class=3D=
"replaceable"><code>&lt;service&gt;</code></em>]
accept  =3D <em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;hostname:portnumber&gt;</co=
de></em>
connect =3D <em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;hostname:portnumber&gt;</co=
de></em></code></pre>
                <p>
                  For a full explanation of the commands and syntax used in
                  the configuration file, issue <span class=3D"command"><st=
rong>man stunnel</strong></span>.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"stunnel-init" name=3D"stunnel-init"></a><s=
pan class=3D"phrase">Boot
                        Script</span>
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  To automatically start the <span class=3D"command"><stron=
g>stunnel</strong></span> daemon when the
                  system is booted, install the <code class=3D"filename">/e=
tc/rc.d/init.d/stunnel</code> bootscript from
                  the <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratc=
h.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#bootscripts" =
title=3D"BLFS Boot Scripts">blfs-bootscripts-20240416</a> package.
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make install-stu=
nnel</kbd></pre>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"content" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Contents
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"segmentedlist">
                <div class=3D"seglistitem">
