          <div class=3D"titlepage">
            <div>
              <div>
                <h2 class=3D"title">
                  <a id=3D"sudo" name=3D"sudo"></a>Sudo-1.9.15p5
                </h2>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class=3D"sect1" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
            <div class=3D"package" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Introduction to Sudo
              </h3>
              <p>
                The <span class=3D"application">Sudo</span> package allows =
a
                system administrator to give certain users (or groups of
                users) the ability to run some (or all) commands as
                <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> or another user whil=
e
                logging the commands and arguments.
              </p>
              <p>
                This package is known to build and work properly using an L=
FS
                12.2 platform.
              </p>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470533767104" name=3D"idm140470533767104"></=
a>Package Information
              </h4>
              <div class=3D"itemizedlist">
                <ul class=3D"compact">
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download (HTTP): <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://w=
ww.sudo.ws/dist/sudo-1.9.15p5.tar.gz">https://www.sudo.ws/dist/sudo-1.9.15p=
5.tar.gz</a>
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download MD5 sum: 4166279cb188ecb6641c7a2ba5f68270
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download size: 5.1 MB
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Estimated disk space required: 53 MB (add 18 MB for
                      tests)
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Estimated build time: 0.2 SBU (with parallelism=3D4; =
add
                      0.1 SBU for tests)
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470533759648" name=3D"idm140470533759648"></=
a>Sudo
                Dependencies
              </h4>
              <h5>
                <a id=3D"idm140470533758960" name=3D"idm140470533758960"></=
a>Optional
              </h5>
              <p class=3D"optional">
                <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/=
blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#linux-pam" title=3D=
"Linux-PAM-1.6.1">Linux-PAM-1.6.1</a>, <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://ww=
w.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.h=
tml#mitkrb" title=3D"MIT Kerberos V5-1.21.3">MIT Kerberos
                V5-1.21.3</a>, <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxf=
romscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#open=
ldap" title=3D"OpenLDAP-2.6.8">OpenLDAP-2.6.8</a>, <a class=3D"xref" href=
=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-s=
ysv-nochunks.html#server-mail" title=3D"Chapter&nbsp;21.&nbsp;Mail Server S=
oftware">MTA</a> (that
                provides a <span class=3D"command"><strong>sendmail</strong=
></span> command),
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://www.openafs.org/">AFS</a=
>,
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://github.com/linux-audit/a=
udit-userspace">libaudit</a>,
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://downloads.sourceforge.ne=
t/opie/">Opie</a>, and
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://sssd.io/">Sssd</a>
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Installation of Sudo
              </h3>
              <p>
                Install <span class=3D"application">Sudo</span> by running =
the
                following commands:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">./configure -=
-prefix=3D/usr              \
            --libexecdir=3D/usr/lib      \
            --with-secure-path         \
            --with-env-editor          \
            --docdir=3D/usr/share/doc/sudo-1.9.15p5 \
            --with-passprompt=3D"[sudo] password for %p: " &amp;&amp;
make</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                To test the results, issue: <span class=3D"command"><strong=
>env
                LC_ALL=3DC make check |&amp; tee
                make-check.log</strong></span>. Check the results with
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>grep failed
                make-check.log</strong></span>.
              </p>
              <p>
                Now, as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make install</kbd>=
</pre>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"commands" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Command Explanations
              </h3>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--libexecdir=3D/usr/lib</code=
></em>: This
                switch controls where private programs are installed.
                Everything in that directory is a library, so they belong
                under <code class=3D"filename">/usr/lib</code> instead of
                <code class=3D"filename">/usr/libexec</code>.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-secure-path</code></em=
>:
                This switch transparently adds <code class=3D"filename">/sb=
in</code> and <code class=3D"filename">/usr/sbin</code> directories to the =
<code class=3D"envar">PATH</code> environment variable.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-env-editor</code></em>=
:
                This switch enables use of the environment variable EDITOR
                for <span class=3D"command"><strong>visudo</strong></span>.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-passprompt</code></em>=
:
                This switch sets the password prompt. The <em class=3D"para=
meter"><code>%p</code></em> will be expanded to the name
                of the user whose password is being requested.
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">--without-pam</code>: This switch av=
oids
                building <span class=3D"application">Linux-PAM</span> suppo=
rt
                when <span class=3D"application">Linux-PAM</span> is instal=
led
                on the system.
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">--with-all-insults</code>: This swit=
ch
                includes all the sudo insult sets. Insults are printed if t=
he
                user types a bad password, and if enabled in <code class=3D=
"filename">/etc/sudoers</code>. Use <code class=3D"option">--with-insults</=
code> to have them enabled by
                default. Various sets of insults can be selected with some
                other switches.
              </p>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  There are many options to <span class=3D"application">sud=
o</span>'s <span class=3D"command"><strong>configure</strong></span> comman=
d. Check
                  the <span class=3D"command"><strong>configure
                  --help</strong></span> output for a complete list.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"configuration" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Configuring Sudo
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"sudo-config" name=3D"sudo-config"></a>Conf=
ig
                        File
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  <code class=3D"filename">/etc/sudoers</code>
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"idm140470533716032" name=3D"idm14047053371=
6032"></a>Configuration Information
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  The <code class=3D"filename">sudoers</code> file can be q=
uite
                  complicated. It is composed of two types of entries:
                  aliases (basically variables) and user specifications
                  (which specify who may run what). The installation instal=
ls
                  a default configuration that has no privileges installed
                  for any user.
                </p>
                <p>
                  A couple of common configuration changes are to set the
                  path for the super user and to allow members of the wheel
                  group to execute all commands after providing their own
                  credentials. Use the following commands to create the
                  <code class=3D"filename">/etc/sudoers.d/00-sudo</code>
                  configuration file as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root=
</code> user:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt; /etc/su=
doers.d/00-sudo &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal">Defaults secure_path=3D"/usr/sbin:/usr/bin"
%wheel ALL=3D(ALL) ALL</code>
EOF</kbd></pre>
                <div class=3D"admon note">
                  <h3>
                    Note
                  </h3>
                  <p>
                    In very simple installations where there is only one
                    user, it may be easier to just edit the <code class=3D"=
filename">/etc/sudoers</code> file directly. In that
                    case, the <code class=3D"varname">secure_path</code> en=
try
                    may not be needed and using <span class=3D"command"><st=
rong>sudo -E ...</strong></span> can import
                    the non-privileged user's full environment into the
                    privileged session.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    The files in the <code class=3D"filename">/etc/sudoers.=
d</code> directory are parsed in
                    sorted lexical order. Be careful that entries in an add=
ed
                    file do not overwrite previous entries.
                  </p>
                </div>
                <p>
                  For details, see <span class=3D"command"><strong>man
                  sudoers</strong></span>.
                </p>
                <div class=3D"admon note">
                  <h3>
                    Note
                  </h3>
                  <p>
                    The <span class=3D"application">Sudo</span> developers
                    highly recommend using the <span class=3D"command"><str=
ong>visudo</strong></span> program to edit
                    the <code class=3D"filename">sudoers</code> file. This =
will
                    provide basic sanity checking like syntax parsing and
                    file permission to avoid some possible mistakes that
                    could lead to a vulnerable configuration.
                  </p>
                </div>
                <p>
                  If <span class=3D"application">PAM</span> is installed on=
 the
                  system, <span class=3D"application">Sudo</span> is built =
with
                  <span class=3D"application">PAM</span> support. In that c=
ase,
                  issue the following command as the <code class=3D"systemi=
tem">root</code> user to create the <span class=3D"application">PAM</span> =
configuration file:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt; /etc/pa=
m.d/sudo &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal"># Begin /etc/pam.d/sudo

# include the default auth settings
auth      include     system-auth

# include the default account settings
account   include     system-account

# Set default environment variables for the service user
session   required    pam_env.so

# include system session defaults
session   include     system-session

# End /etc/pam.d/sudo</code>
EOF
chmod 644 /etc/pam.d/sudo</kbd></pre>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"content" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Contents
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"segmentedlist">
                <div class=3D"seglistitem">
