            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Installation of GRUB
              </h3>
              <p>
                First, install font data as the <code class=3D"systemitem">=
root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">mkdir -pv /usr/sha=
re/fonts/unifont &amp;&amp;
gunzip -c ../unifont-15.1.05.pcf.gz &gt; /usr/share/fonts/unifont/unifont.p=
cf</kbd></pre>
              <div class=3D"admon warning">
                <h3>
                  Warning
                </h3>
                <p>
                  Unset any environment variables which may affect the buil=
d:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">unset {C,CP=
P,CXX,LD}FLAGS</kbd></pre>
                <p>
                  Don't try <span class=3D"quote">=E2=80=9C<span class=3D"q=
uote">tuning</span>=E2=80=9D</span> this package with custom
                  compilation flags: this package is a bootloader, with
                  low-level operations in the source code which is likely t=
o
                  be broken by some aggressive optimizations.
                </p>
              </div>
              <p>
                Add a file missing from the release tarball:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">echo <code cl=
ass=3D"literal">depends bli part_gpt</code> &gt; grub-core/extra_deps.lst</=
kbd></pre>
              <p>
                If you are running a 32-bit LFS, prepare a 64-bit compiler:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">case $(uname =
-m) in i?86 )
    <code class=3D"literal">tar xf ../gcc-14.2.0.tar.xz
    mkdir gcc-14.2.0/build
    pushd gcc-14.2.0/build
        ../configure --prefix=3D$PWD/../../x86_64-gcc \
                     --target=3Dx86_64-linux-gnu      \
                     --with-system-zlib             \
                     --enable-languages=3Dc,c++       \
                     --with-ld=3D/usr/bin/ld
        make all-gcc
        make install-gcc
    popd
    export TARGET_CC=3D$PWD/x86_64-gcc/bin/x86_64-linux-gnu-gcc</code>
esac</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                Build <span class=3D"application">GRUB</span> with the
                following commands:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">./configure -=
-prefix=3D/usr        \
            --sysconfdir=3D/etc    \
            --disable-efiemu     \
            --enable-grub-mkfont \
            --with-platform=3Defi  \
            --target=3Dx86_64      \
            --disable-werror     &amp;&amp;
unset TARGET_CC &amp;&amp;
make</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                This package does not have a test suite providing meaningfu=
l
                results.
              </p>
              <p>
                Now, if you've skip the LFS GRUB package, as the <code clas=
s=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">make install =
&amp;&amp;
mv -v /etc/bash_completion.d/grub /usr/share/bash-completion/completions</k=
bd></pre>
              <p>
                If you've not skip LFS GRUB package, as the <code class=3D"=
systemitem">root</code> user, only install the components
                not installed from the LFS GRUB package instead:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make DESTDIR=3D$PW=
D/dest install
cp -av dest/usr/lib/grub/x86_64-efi -T /usr/lib/grub/x86_64-efi
cp -av dest/usr/share/grub/*.{pf2,h}   /usr/share/grub
cp -av dest/usr/bin/grub-mkfont        /usr/bin</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                If the optional dependencies are installed, also install th=
e
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>grub-mount</strong></span>
                program:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">cp -av dest/u=
sr/bin/grub-mount /usr/bin</kbd></pre>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"commands" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Command Explanations
              </h3>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--enable-grub-mkfont</code></=
em>:
                Build the tool named <span class=3D"command"><strong>grub-m=
kfont</strong></span> to generate the
                font file for the boot loader from the font data we've
                installed.
              </p>
              <div class=3D"admon warning">
                <h3>
                  Warning
                </h3>
                <p>
                  If the recommended dependency <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"h=
ttps://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-n=
ochunks.html#freetype2" title=3D"FreeType-2.13.3">FreeType-2.13.3</a> is
                  not installed, it is possible to omit this option and bui=
ld
                  GRUB. However, if <span class=3D"command"><strong>grub-mk=
font</strong></span> is not built,
                  or the unicode font data is not available at the time GRU=
B
                  is built, GRUB won't install any font for the boot loader=
.
                  The GRUB boot menu will be displayed using a coarse font =
or
                  in a smaller region on the screen.
                </p>
              </div>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-platform=3Defi</code><=
/em>:
                Ensures building GRUB with EFI enabled.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--target=3Dx86_64</code></em>=
:
                Ensures building GRUB for x86_64 even if building on a 32-b=
it
                LFS system. Most EFI firmware on x86_64 does not support
                32-bit bootloaders.
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">--target=3Di386</code>: A few 32-bit=
 x86
                platforms have EFI support. And, some x86_64 platforms have=
 a
                32-bit EFI implementation, but they are very old and rare.
                Use this instead of <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--target=
=3Dx86_64</code></em> if you are
                <span class=3D"bold"><strong>absolutely sure</strong></span=
>
                that LFS is running on such a system.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"sect2" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Configuring GRUB
              </h3>
              <p>
                Using GRUB to make the LFS system bootable on UEFI platform
                will be discussed in <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.=
linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.htm=
l#grub-setup" title=3D"Using GRUB to Set Up the Boot Process with UEFI">Usi=
ng
                GRUB to Set Up the Boot Process with UEFI</a>.
              </p>
            </div>
