                    <col>
                  </colgroup>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"dash-prog" name=3D"dash-prog"></a><span =
class=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>dash</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          is a POSIX compliant shell
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class=3D"titlepage">
            <div>
              <div>
                <h2 class=3D"title">
                  <a id=3D"tcsh" name=3D"tcsh"></a>Tcsh-6.24.13
                </h2>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class=3D"sect1" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
            <div class=3D"package" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Introduction to Tcsh
              </h3>
              <p>
                The <span class=3D"application">Tcsh</span> package contain=
s
                <span class=3D"quote">=E2=80=9C<span class=3D"quote">an enh=
anced but
                completely compatible version of the Berkeley Unix C shell
                (<span class=3D"command"><strong>csh</strong></span>).</spa=
n>=E2=80=9D</span> This
                is useful as an alternative shell for those who prefer C
                syntax to that of the <span class=3D"command"><strong>bash<=
/strong></span> shell, and also
                because some programs require the C shell in order to perfo=
rm
                installation tasks.
              </p>
              <p>
                This package is known to build and work properly using an L=
FS
                12.2 platform.
              </p>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470531265136" name=3D"idm140470531265136"></=
a>Package Information
              </h4>
              <div class=3D"itemizedlist">
                <ul class=3D"compact">
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download (HTTP): <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://a=
stron.com/pub/tcsh/tcsh-6.24.13.tar.gz">https://astron.com/pub/tcsh/tcsh-6.=
24.13.tar.gz</a>
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download MD5 sum: 7467f2a08b758d3439aad332322e6a98
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download size: 940 KB
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Estimated disk space required: 11 MB (with tests)
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Estimated build time: 0.2 SBU (with tests)
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Installation of Tcsh
              </h3>
              <p>
                Install <span class=3D"application">Tcsh</span> by running =
the
                following commands:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">./configure -=
-prefix=3D/usr &amp;&amp;
make</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                To test the results, issue: <span class=3D"command"><strong=
>make check</strong></span>.
              </p>
              <p>
                Now, as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make install insta=
ll.man &amp;&amp;

ln -v -sf tcsh   /bin/csh &amp;&amp;
ln -v -sf tcsh.1 /usr/share/man/man1/csh.1</kbd></pre>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"commands" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Command Explanations
              </h3>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>ln -v -sf tcsh
                /bin/csh</strong></span>: The FHS states that if there is a
                <span class=3D"application">C</span> shell installed, there
                should be a symlink from <code class=3D"filename">/bin/csh<=
/code> to it. This creates that symlink.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"configuration" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Configuring Tcsh
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"tcsh-config" name=3D"tcsh-config"></a>Conf=
ig
                        Files
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  There are numerous configuration files for the C shell.
                  Examples of these are <code class=3D"filename">/etc/csh.c=
shrc</code>, <code class=3D"filename">/etc/csh.login</code>, <code class=3D=
"filename">/etc/csh.logout</code>, <code class=3D"filename">~/.tcshrc</code=
>, <code class=3D"filename">~/.cshrc</code>, <code class=3D"filename">~/.hi=
story</code>, <code class=3D"filename">~/.cshdirs</code>, <code class=3D"fi=
lename">~/.login</code>, and <code class=3D"filename">~/.logout</code>. Mor=
e information on these
                  files can be found in the <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"http=
s://man.archlinux.org/man/tcsh.1">tcsh(1)</a> man
                  page.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"idm140470531219072" name=3D"idm14047053121=
9072"></a>Configuration Information
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  Update <code class=3D"filename">/etc/shells</code> to inc=
lude
                  the C shell program names (as the <code class=3D"systemit=
em">root</code> user):
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt;&gt; /et=
c/shells &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal">/bin/tcsh
/bin/csh</code>
EOF</kbd></pre>
                <p>
                  The following <code class=3D"filename">~/.cshrc</code>
                  provides two alternative colour prompts and coloured
                  <span class=3D"command"><strong>ls</strong></span> output=
. If
                  you prefer a global modification, issue the command as th=
e
                  <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user, replacing
                  <code class=3D"filename">~/.cshrc</code> by <code class=
=3D"filename">/etc/csh.cshrc</code>.
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt; ~/=
.cshrc &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal"># Original at:
# https://www.cs.umd.edu/~srhuang/teaching/code_snippets/prompt_color.tcsh.=
html

# Modified by the BLFS Development Team.

# Add these lines to your ~/.cshrc (or to /etc/csh.cshrc).

# Colors!
set     red=3D"%{\033[1;31m%}"
set   green=3D"%{\033[0;32m%}"
set  yellow=3D"%{\033[1;33m%}"
set    blue=3D"%{\033[1;34m%}"
set magenta=3D"%{\033[1;35m%}"
set    cyan=3D"%{\033[1;36m%}"
set   white=3D"%{\033[0;37m%}"
set     end=3D"%{\033[0m%}" # This is needed at the end...

# Setting the actual prompt.  Two separate versions for you to try, pick
# whichever one you like better, and change the colors as you want.
# Just don't mess with the ${end} guy in either line...  Comment out or
# delete the prompt you don't use.

set prompt=3D"${green}%n${blue}@%m ${white}%~ ${green}%%${end} "
set prompt=3D"[${green}%n${blue}@%m ${white}%~ ]${end} "

# This was not in the original URL above
# Provides coloured ls
alias ls ls --color=3Dalways

# Clean up after ourselves...
unset red green yellow blue magenta cyan yellow white end</code>
EOF</kbd></pre>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"content" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Contents
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"segmentedlist">
                <div class=3D"seglistitem">
                  <div class=3D"seg">
