blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#libpng" title=3D"li=
bpng-1.6.43">libpng-1.6.43</a>, <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linux=
fromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#lib=
tasn1" title=3D"libtasn1-4.19.0">libtasn1-4.19.0</a>,
                <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/=
blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#linux-pam" title=3D=
"Linux-PAM-1.6.1">Linux-PAM-1.6.1</a>, <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://ww=
w.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.h=
tml#lzo" title=3D"LZO-2.10">LZO-2.10</a>, <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https:/=
/www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunk=
s.html#nettle" title=3D"Nettle-3.10">Nettle-3.10</a>, <a class=3D"xref" hre=
f=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-=
sysv-nochunks.html#mesa" title=3D"Mesa-24.1.5">Mesa-24.1.5</a>,
                <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/=
blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#vte" title=3D"VTE-0=
.76.4">VTE-0.76.4</a>, <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://www.capstone-engi=
ne.org/">capstone</a>, <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://github.com/ceph/c=
eph/">ceph</a>,
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://pmem.io/daxctl/">daxctl<=
/a>,
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://jackaudio.org/">JACK</a>=
,
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://github.com/gluster/glust=
erfs">glusterfs</a>,
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://github.com/libbpf/libbpf=
">libbpf</a>, <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/spi=
ce/libcacard">libcacard</a>,
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://people.redhat.com/sgrubb=
/libcap-ng/">libcap-ng</a>,
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://sourceware.org/elfutils/=
">libdw</a>, <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://github.com/sahlberg/libiscs=
i">libiscsi</a>,
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://github.com/sahlberg/libn=
fs">libnfs</a>, <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://pmem.io/pmdk/libpmem/">l=
ibpmem</a>,
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://www.libssh.org/">libssh<=
/a>,
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://github.com/Agnoctopus/li=
bu2f-emu">libu2f-emu</a>,
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://github.com/lzfse/lzfse">=
lzfse</a>, <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://github.com/luigirizzo/netmap"=
>netmap</a>,
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://github.com/numactl/numac=
tl">numactl</a>, <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://github.com/linux-rdma/r=
dma-core">rdma-core</a>,
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://selinuxproject.org/">SEL=
inux</a>, <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://google.github.io/snappy/">snap=
py</a>, <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/spice/spi=
ce">spice</a>,
                <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/s=
pice/usbredir">usbredir</a>,
                and <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://github.com/virtualsq=
uare/vde-2">VDE</a>
              </p>
              <h5>
                <a id=3D"idm140470531039968" name=3D"idm140470531039968"></=
a>Optional (Runtime)
              </h5>
              <p class=3D"runtime">
                <span class=3D"phrase"><a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://ww=
w.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.h=
tml#elogind" title=3D"elogind-255.5">elogind-255.5</a></span>
              </p>
              <h5>
                <a id=3D"idm140470531037536" name=3D"idm140470531037536"></=
a>Optional (for building the
                documentation)
              </h5>
              <p class=3D"optional">
                <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/=
blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#sphinx_rtd_theme" t=
itle=3D"Sphinx_rtd_theme-2.0.0">sphinx_rtd_theme-2.0.0</a>
              </p>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  This optional dependencies list is not comprehensive. See
                  the output of <span class=3D"command"><strong>./configure
                  --help</strong></span> for a more complete list.
                </p>
              </div>
              <p class=3D"usernotes">
                Editor Notes: <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://wiki.linux=
fromscratch.org/blfs/wiki/qemu">https://wiki.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/wiki=
/qemu</a>
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"sect2" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                <a id=3D"qemu-prereq" name=3D"qemu-prereq"></a>KVM Prerequi=
sites
              </h3>
              <p>
                Before building <span class=3D"application">qemu</span>, ch=
eck
                to see if your processor supports Virtualization Technology
                (VT):
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">grep -E '^fla=
gs.*(vmx|svm)' /proc/cpuinfo</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                If you get any output, you have VT technology (vmx for Inte=
l
                processors and svm for AMD processors). You then need to go
                into your system BIOS and ensure it is enabled. After
                enabling, reboot back to your LFS instance.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"kernel" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                <a id=3D"qemu-kernel" name=3D"qemu-kernel"></a>Kernel
                Configuration
              </h3>
              <p>
                Enable the following options in the kernel configuration an=
d
                recompile the kernel if necessary:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"screen">[*] <span class=3D"blue">V</span>irtual=
ization ---&gt;                                         [VIRTUALIZATION]
  &lt;*/M&gt;   <span class=3D"blue">K</span>ernel-based Virtual Machine (K=
VM) support                       [KVM]
  # Enable the option for your CPU:
  &lt; /*/M&gt;   <span class=3D"blue">K</span>VM for Intel (and compatible=
) processors support        [KVM_INTEL]
  &lt; /*/M&gt;   <span class=3D"blue">K</span>VM for AMD processors suppor=
t                             [KVM_AMD]</pre>
              <p>
                The Intel or AMD settings are not both required, but the on=
e
                matching your system processor is required.
              </p>
              <p>
                To use the <span class=3D"quote">=E2=80=9C<span class=3D"qu=
ote">bridge</span>=E2=80=9D</span> network device, as explained
                below, check that <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.lin=
uxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#b=
ridgeutils" title=3D"bridge-utils-1.7.1">bridge-utils-1.7.1</a> is installe=
d and
                the following options in the kernel configuration are
                enabled:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"screen">[*] N<span class=3D"blue">e</span>twork=
ing support ---&gt;                                                [NET]
  N<span class=3D"blue">e</span>tworking options ---&gt;
    &lt;*/M&gt; 802.1<span class=3D"blue">d</span> Ethernet Bridging       =
                               [BRIDGE]

<span class=3D"blue">D</span>evice Drivers ---&gt;
  [*] N<span class=3D"blue">e</span>twork device support ---&gt;           =
                        [NETDEVICES]
    [*]   N<span class=3D"blue">e</span>twork core driver support          =
                       [NET_CORE]
    &lt;*/M&gt;   <span class=3D"blue">U</span>niversal TUN/TAP device driv=
er support                        [TUN]</pre>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Installation of qemu
              </h3>
              <p>
                The udev rule of LFS only allows the <code class=3D"systemi=
tem">root</code> user, the users owning a local login
                session supported by the optional runtime dependency
                <span class=3D"phrase"><a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://ww=
w.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.h=
tml#elogind" title=3D"elogind-255.5">elogind-255.5</a></span> , or the user=
s in
                the <code class=3D"systemitem">kvm</code> group to use the =
KVM
                device. As the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user,=
 add
                any non-<code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> users that m=
ight
                use the KVM device either without <span class=3D"phrase"><a=
 class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/sta=
ble/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#elogind" title=3D"elogind-255.5">elog=
ind-255.5</a></span> installed or
                remotely (via a SSH connection) to the <code class=3D"syste=
mitem">kvm</code> group:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">usermod -a -G kvm =
<em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;username&gt;</code></em></kbd></pre>
              <p>
                Install <span class=3D"application">qemu</span> by running =
the
                following commands:
              </p>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  Qemu is capable of running many targets. The build proces=
s
                  is also capable of building multiple targets at one time =
in
                  a comma delimited list assigned to <code class=3D"option"=
>--target-list</code>. Run <span class=3D"command"><strong>./configure --he=
lp</strong></span> to get
                  a complete list of available targets.
                </p>
              </div>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">if [ $(uname =
-m) =3D i686 ]; then
   QEMU_ARCH=3Di386-softmmu
else
   QEMU_ARCH=3Dx86_64-softmmu
fi


mkdir -vp build &amp;&amp;
cd        build &amp;&amp;

../configure --prefix=3D/usr               \
             --sysconfdir=3D/etc           \
             --localstatedir=3D/var        \
             --target-list=3D$QEMU_ARCH    \
             --audio-drv-list=3Dalsa       \
             --disable-pa                \
             --enable-slirp              \
             --docdir=3D/usr/share/doc/qemu-9.0.2 &amp;&amp;

unset QEMU_ARCH &amp;&amp;

make</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"application">qemu</span> uses <span class=3D=
"command"><strong>ninja</strong></span> as a subprocess when
                building. To run the tests, issue: <span class=3D"command">=
<strong>ninja test</strong></span>. One test,
                bios-tables-test, is known to fail.
              </p>
              <p>
                Now, as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make install</kbd>=
</pre>
              <p>
                Change the permissions and ownership of a helper script,
                which is needed when using the <span class=3D"quote">=E2=80=
=9C<span class=3D"quote">bridge</span>=E2=80=9D</span> network
                device (see below). Again as the <code class=3D"systemitem"=
>root</code> user, issue:
              </p>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  You need to add any users who might use the <span class=
=3D"quote">=E2=80=9C<span class=3D"quote">bridge</span>=E2=80=9D</span> net=
work
                  device into the <code class=3D"systemitem">kvm</code> gro=
up
                  even if <span class=3D"phrase"><a class=3D"xref" href=3D"=
https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-=
nochunks.html#elogind" title=3D"elogind-255.5">elogind-255.5</a></span>
                  is installed.
                </p>
              </div>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">chgrp kvm  /usr/li=
bexec/qemu-bridge-helper &amp;&amp;
chmod 4750 /usr/libexec/qemu-bridge-helper</kbd></pre>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  For convenience you may want to create a symbolic link to
                  run the installed program. For instance (as the
                  <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user):
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">ln -sv qemu-syst=
em-`uname -m` /usr/bin/qemu</kbd></pre>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"commands" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Command Explanations
              </h3>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--audio-drv-list=3Dalsa</code=
></em>: This
                switch sets the audio driver to ALSA. See below for enablin=
g
                other audio drivers.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--disable-pa</code></em>: eve=
n if
                <span class=3D"emphasis"><em>pa</em></span> is not in
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--audio-drv-list</code></em>
                list, the pulseaudio driver is built, unless disabled by th=
is
                parameter.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--enable-slirp</code></em>: T=
his
                switch forces the building system to check for <a class=3D"=
xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-B=
OOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#libslirp" title=3D"libslirp-4.8.0">libslirp-4.8=
.0</a>. Remove it if you don't
                need the <code class=3D"option">-netdev user</code> support=
.
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">--audio-drv-list=3Dpa
                --disable-alsa</code>: This switch sets the audio driver to
                pulseaudio. For other drivers see the --audio-drv-list
                choices in the output of <span class=3D"command"><strong>./=
configure --help</strong></span>. The
                default audio driver is OSS. To enable support for both als=
a
                and pulseaudio, use <code class=3D"option">--audio-drv-list=
=3Dalsa,pa</code>.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"using" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Using Qemu
              </h3>
              <p>
                Since using qemu means using a virtual computer, the steps =
to
                set up the virtual machine are in close analogy with those =
to
                set up a real computer. You'll need to decide about CPU,
                memory, disk, USB devices, network card(s), screen size, et=
c.
                Once the <span class=3D"quote">=E2=80=9C<span class=3D"quot=
e">hardware</span>=E2=80=9D</span> is decided, you'll have for
                example to choose how to connect the machine to internet,
                and/or to install an OS. In the following, we show basic wa=
ys
                of performing those steps. But qemu is much more than this,
                and it is strongly advised to read the qemu documentation i=
n
                <code class=3D"filename">/usr/share/doc/qemu-9.0.2/qemu-doc=
.html</code>.
              </p>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  It is standard practice to name the computer running qemu
                  <span class=3D"quote">=E2=80=9C<span class=3D"quote">host=
</span>=E2=80=9D</span> and the emulated machine
                  running under qemu the <span class=3D"quote">=E2=80=9C<sp=
an class=3D"quote">guest.</span>=E2=80=9D</span> We'll use those notations =
in
                  the following.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  The following instructions assume the optional symbolic
                  link, <code class=3D"filename">qemu</code>, has been crea=
ted.
                  Additionally, <span class=3D"command"><strong>qemu</stron=
g></span> should be run in a
                  graphical environment. But it is possible to use qemu
                  <span class=3D"quote">=E2=80=9C<span class=3D"quote">head=
less</span>=E2=80=9D</span> or through SSH. See the
                  documentation for the various possibilities.
                </p>
              </div>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470530962544" name=3D"idm140470530962544"></=
a>Disk
              </h4>
              <p>
                A virtual disk may be set up in the following way:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">VDISK_SIZE=3D=
<em class=3D"replaceable"><code>50G</code></em>
VDISK_FILENAME=3D<em class=3D"replaceable"><code>vdisk.img</code></em>
qemu-img create -f qcow2 $VDISK_FILENAME $VDISK_SIZE</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                The virtual disk size and filename should be adjusted as
                desired. The actual size of the file will be less than
                specified, but will expand as needed, so it is safe to put =
a
                high value.
              </p>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470530958752" name=3D"idm140470530958752"></=
a>Operating System
              </h4>
              <p>
                To install an operating system, download an iso image from
                your preferred Linux distribution. For the purposes of this
                example, we'll use <code class=3D"filename">Fedora-16-x86_6=
4-Live-LXDE.iso</code> in the
                current directory. Run the following:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">qemu -enable-=
kvm                           \
     -drive file=3D$VDISK_FILENAME           \
     -cdrom Fedora-16-x86_64-Live-LXDE.iso \
     -boot d                               \
     -m <em class=3D"replaceable"><code>1G</code></em></kbd></pre>
              <p>
                Follow the normal installation procedures for the chosen
                distribution. The <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-boot</code=
></em> option specifies the boot
                order of drives as a string of drive letters. Valid drive
                letters are: a, b (floppy 1 and 2), c (first hard disk), d
                (first CD-ROM). The <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-m</code>=
</em> option is the amount of
                memory to use for the virtual machine. The choice depends o=
n
                the load of the host. Modern distributions should be
                comfortable with 1GB. The <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-en=
able-kvm</code></em> option allows
                hardware acceleration. Without this switch, the emulation i=
s
                much slower.
              </p>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470530952176" name=3D"idm140470530952176"></=
a>Defining the virtual hardware
              </h4>
              <p>
                The virtual machine hardware is defined by the qemu command
                line. An example command is given below:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">qemu -enable-=
kvm                     \
     -smp 4                          \
     -cpu host                       \
     -m 1G                           \
     -drive file=3D$VDISK_FILENAME     \
     -cdrom grub-img.iso             \
     -boot order=3Dc,once=3Dd,menu=3Don    \
     -net nic,netdev=3Dnet0            \
     -netdev user,id=3Dnet0            \
     -device ac97                    \
     -vga std                        \
     -serial mon:stdio               \
     -name "fedora-16"</kbd></pre>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470530949904" name=3D"idm140470530949904"></=
a>Meaning of the command line options
              </h4>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-enable-kvm</code></em>: enab=
le
                full KVM virtualization support. On some hardware, it may b=
e
                necessary to add the undocumented <code class=3D"option">-m=
achine smm=3Doff</code> option in order to enable
                KVM.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-smp &lt;N&gt;</code></em>:
                enable symmetric multiprocessing with &lt;N&gt; CPUs.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-cpu &lt;model&gt;</code></em=
>:
                simulate CPU &lt;model&gt;. the list of supported models ca=
n
                be obtained with <code class=3D"option">-cpu help</code>.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-drive
                file=3D&lt;filename&gt;</code></em>: defines a virtual disk
                whose image is stored in <code class=3D"filename">&lt;filen=
ame&gt;</code>.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-cdrom grub-img.iso</code></e=
m>:
                defines an iso formatted file to use as a cdrom. Here we us=
e
                a grub rescue disk, which may turn handy when something goe=
s
                wrong at boot time.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-boot
                order=3Dc,once=3Dd,menu=3Don</code></em>: defines the boot =
order
                for the virtual BIOS.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-net
                nic,netdev=3D&lt;netid&gt;</code></em>: defines a network c=
ard
                connected to the network device with id &lt;netid&gt;.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-netdev
                user,id=3D&lt;netid&gt;</code></em>: defines the network
                <span class=3D"quote">=E2=80=9C<span class=3D"quote">user</=
span>=E2=80=9D</span>
                device. This is a virtual local network with addresses
                10.0.2.0/24, where the host has address 10.0.2.2 and acts a=
s
                a gateway to internet, and with a name server at address
                10.0.2.3, and an smb server at address 10.0.2.4. A builtin
                DHCP server can allocate addresses between 10.0.2.15 and
                10.0.2.31.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-soundhw
                &lt;model&gt;</code></em>: defines the soundcard model. The
                list may be obtained with <code class=3D"option">-soundhw
                help</code>.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-vga &lt;type&gt;</code></em>=
:
                defines the type of VGA card to emulate. For <em class=3D"p=
arameter"><code>-vga std</code></em>, if you are building a
                Linux kernel for the guest, it's recommended to enable
                <code class=3D"option">CONFIG_DRM_BOCHS</code> (as a part o=
f
                the kernel or a kernel module) to drive all the features of
                the emulated VGA card, and <code class=3D"option">CONFIG_FB=
</code> to display the Linux console on it.
                The other <code class=3D"option">&lt;type&gt;</code> values=
 are
                not tested by the editors and may require additional
                dependencies.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-serial mon:stdio</code></em>=
:
                sends the serial port of the guest (<code class=3D"filename=
">/dev/ttyS0</code> on linux guests), multiplexed
                with the qemu monitor, to the standard input and output of
                the qemu process.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-name &lt;name&gt;</code></em=
>:
                sets the name of the guest. This name is displayed in the
                guest window caption. It may be useful if you run several
                guests at the same time.
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">-drive
                if=3Dpflash,format=3Draw,readonly=3Don,file=3D/usr/share/qe=
mu/edk2-x86_64-code.fd</code>:
                Load a pre-built EDK2 UEFI firmware, instead of the default
                PC BIOS. Use this option if you want to boot the guest OS
                with UEFI.
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">-drive
                file=3D&lt;filename&gt;,if=3Dvirtio</code>: Provide Virtio
                interface to the guest kernel for accessing the disk image,
                instead of simulating a real disk hardware. This can improv=
e
                disk I/O performance, but it requires a Virtio driver in
                guest kernel. Use it instead of a plain <code class=3D"opti=
on">-drive</code> if the guest kernel supports Virtio.
                To build a Linux kernel with Virtio support for the guest,
                use <span class=3D"command"><strong>make defconfig &amp;&am=
p;
                make kvm_guest.config</strong></span> to create an initial
                kernel configuration with the Virtio drives enabled, then
                make your customization. And, if the guest kernel is Linux,
                the virtual disks using Virtio interface will be named
                <code class=3D"filename">vdx</code> in the devtmpfs, instea=
d of
                <code class=3D"filename">sdx</code>.
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">-net
                nic,netdev=3Dnet0,model=3Dvirtio-net-pci</code>: Provide Vi=
rtio
                interface to the guest kernel for accessing the network
                interface, instead of simulating a real network interface
                card. This can improve network I/O performance, but it
                requires a Virtio driver in guest kernel. Use it instead of=
 a
                plain <code class=3D"option">-net</code> if the guest kerne=
l
                supports Virtio.
              </p>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470530922432" name=3D"idm140470530922432"></=
a>Controlling the Emulated Display
              </h4>
              <p>
                To set the resolution of the emulated display for a Xorg
                server running in the guest Linux system, read <a class=3D"=
xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-B=
OOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#xdisplay" title=3D"Fine Tuning Display Settings=
">the section called =E2=80=9CFine
                Tuning Display Settings=E2=80=9D</a>.
              </p>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470530920432" name=3D"idm140470530920432"></=
a>Networking
              </h4>
              <p>
                The above solution for networking allows the guest to acces=
s
                the local network through the host (and possibly to access
                internet through the local routers), but the converse is no=
t
                true. Not even the host can access the guest, unless port
                forwarding is enabled. And in the case several guests are
                running, they cannot communicate with each other. Other
                network devices can be used for this purpose. For example,
                there is the <span class=3D"quote">=E2=80=9C<span class=3D"=
quote">socket</span>=E2=80=9D</span> device, which allows several
                guests to share a common virtual network. In the following,
                we describe in more details how to set up the <span class=
=3D"quote">=E2=80=9C<span class=3D"quote">bridge</span>=E2=80=9D</span> dev=
ice,
                which allows the guests to appear as if connected to the
                local network. All the commands below should be run as the
                <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user.
              </p>
              <p>
                Set up bridging with <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.=
linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.htm=
l#bridgeutils" title=3D"bridge-utils-1.7.1">bridge-utils-1.7.1</a>. Only th=
e
                physical interface(s) should be set up at boot. The virtual
                interface(s) will be added as needed when qemu is started.
              </p>
              <p>
                Set up a required configuration file:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">install -vdm 755 /=
etc/qemu &amp;&amp;
echo allow br0 &gt; /etc/qemu/bridge.conf</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                In the qemu command line above, replace the switch <em clas=
s=3D"parameter"><code>-netdev user,...</code></em> with
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-netdev bridge,...</code></em=
>.
              </p>
            </div>
