            <div class=3D"kernel" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Configuring Libusb
              </h3>
              <p>
                <a id=3D"libusb-kernel" name=3D"libusb-kernel"></a> To acce=
ss raw
                USB devices (those not treated as a disk by the mass-storag=
e
                driver), appropriate support must be available in the kerne=
l.
                Check your kernel configuration:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"screen"><span class=3D"blue">D</span>evice Driv=
ers ---&gt;
  [*] <span class=3D"blue">U</span>SB support ---&gt;                      =
                       [USB_SUPPORT]
    &lt;*/M&gt;   <span class=3D"blue">S</span>upport for Host-side USB    =
                                  [USB]
    [*]     <span class=3D"blue">P</span>CI based USB host interface       =
                        [USB_PCI]
    # These are most common USB controller drivers for PC-like systems.
    # For modern systems often [USB_XHCI_HCD] is the only one needed
    # even if the system has USB 2.0 ports:
    &lt; /*/M&gt; <span class=3D"blue">x</span>HCI HCD (USB 3.0) support   =
                         [USB_XHCI_HCD]
    &lt; /*/M&gt; <span class=3D"blue">E</span>HCI HCD (USB 2.0) support   =
                         [USB_EHCI_HCD]
    &lt; /*/M&gt; <span class=3D"blue">O</span>HCI HCD (USB 1.1) support   =
                         [USB_OHCI_HCD]</pre>
              <p>
                For more details on setting up USB devices, see <a class=3D=
"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-=
BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#usb-device-issues" title=3D"USB Device Issues"=
>the section called =E2=80=9CUSB Device
                Issues=E2=80=9D</a>.
              </p>
