            </div>
            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Installation of Graphviz
              </h3>
              <p>
                Install <span class=3D"application">Graphviz</span> by runn=
ing
                the following commands:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">sed -i '/LIBP=
OSTFIX=3D"64"/s/64//' configure.ac &amp;&amp;

./autogen.sh              &amp;&amp;
./configure --prefix=3D/usr \
            --docdir=3D/usr/share/doc/graphviz-12.1.0</kbd></pre>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  A warning is generated by <span class=3D"command"><strong=
>autogen.sh</strong></span> because the
                  build tree is not a git repository. As a result, the buil=
d
                  date is set to 0. To get a meaningful date in the version
                  string, you can run:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">sed -i "s/0=
/$(date +%Y%m%d)/" builddate.h</kbd></pre>
              </div>
              <p>
                Whether or not you fix the date, proceed to compile the
                package:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">make</kbd></p=
re>
              <p>
                This package does not come with a test suite that provides
                meaningful results.
              </p>
              <p>
                Now, as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make install</kbd>=
</pre>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"commands" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Command Explanations
              </h3>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>sed ...
                configure.ac</strong></span>: This command is needed to avo=
id
                installing files in /usr/lib64.
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">--with-javaincludedir=3D"$JAVA_HOME/=
include
                -I$JAVA_HOME/include/linux"</code>: If you have built
                <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/=
blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#openjdk" title=3D"O=
penJDK-22.0.2">OpenJDK-22.0.2</a> in /opt, and you want to
                build the JAVA bindings, it is necessary to specify the
                location of the JAVA header files to configure. The configu=
re
                switch is designed for only one directory, but two
                directories need to be included. This is possible
                nevertheless by using the -I switch inside the variable.
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">--with-webp</code>: Even if <a class=
=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BL=
FS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#libwebp" title=3D"libwebp-1.4.0">libwebp-1.=
4.0</a> is installed, it is not
                included in the build without this option.
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">--with-smyrna</code>: Even if the ne=
eded
                dependencies are installed, the interactive graph viewer
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>smyrna</strong></span> is n=
ot
                built without this option.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"configuration" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Configuring Graphviz
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"graphviz-config" name=3D"graphviz-config">=
</a>Config Files
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  <code class=3D"filename">/usr/lib/graphviz/config</code>
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"idm140470522548704" name=3D"idm14047052254=
8704"></a>Configuration Information
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  There are no specific configuration requirements for
                  <span class=3D"application">Graphviz</span>. You may cons=
ider
                  installing the additional plugins and tools available fro=
m
                  the download page at <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://g=
raphviz.org/download/source/">https://graphviz.org/download/source/</a>
                  for additional capabilities. If additional plugins are
                  installed, you can run <span class=3D"command"><strong>do=
t
                  -c</strong></span> (as the <code class=3D"systemitem">roo=
t</code> user) to update the <code class=3D"filename">config</code> file in=
 <code class=3D"filename">/usr/lib/graphviz</code>.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"content" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Contents
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"segmentedlist">
                <div class=3D"seglistitem">
                  <div class=3D"seg">
                    <strong class=3D"segtitle">Installed Programs:</strong>
                    <span class=3D"segbody">acyclic, bcomps, ccomps, circo,
                    cluster, dijkstra, dot, dot2gxl, dot_builtins, edgepain=
t,
                    fdp, gc, gml2gv, graphml2gv, gv2gml, gv2gxl, gvcolor,
                    gvedit, gvgen, gvmap, gvmap.sh, gvpack, gvpr, gxl2dot,
                    gxl2gv, mm2gv, neato, nop, osage, patchwork, prune,
                    sccmap, sfdp, tred, twopi, unflatten, and vimdot</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class=3D"seg">
                    <strong class=3D"segtitle">Installed Libraries:</strong=
>
                    <span class=3D"segbody">libcdt.so, libcgraph.so, libgvc=
.so,
                    libgvpr.so, liblab_gamut.so, libpathplan.so, libxdot.so=
,
                    and several plugins in /usr/lib/graphviz. There are als=
o
                    several in subdirectories of
                    /usr/lib/{lua,perl5,php,python3.12,tcl8.6}.
                    Unfortunately, some libraries are duplicated.</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class=3D"seg">
                    <strong class=3D"segtitle">Installed Directories:</stro=
ng>
                    <span class=3D"segbody">/usr/include/graphviz,
                    /usr/lib/graphviz, /usr/lib/tcl8.6/graphviz,
                    /usr/share/doc/graphviz-12.1.0, and
                    /usr/share/graphviz</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"variablelist">
                <h4>
                  <a id=3D"idm140470522537472" name=3D"idm140470522537472">=
</a>Short Descriptions
                </h4>
                <table border=3D"0" class=3D"variablelist">
                  <colgroup>
                    <col align=3D"left" valign=3D"top">
                    <col>
                  </colgroup>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"acyclic" name=3D"acyclic"></a><span clas=
s=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>acyclic</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          is a filter that takes a directed graph as input
                          and outputs a copy of the graph with sufficient
                          edges reversed to make the graph acyclic
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"bcomps" name=3D"bcomps"></a><span class=
=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>bcomps</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          decomposes graphs into their biconnected
                          components, printing the components to standard
                          output
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"ccomps" name=3D"ccomps"></a><span class=
=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>ccomps</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          decomposes graphs into their connected components=
,
                          printing the components to standard output
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"circo" name=3D"circo"></a><span class=3D=
"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>circo</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          draws graphs using a circular layout
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"cluster" name=3D"cluster"></a><span clas=
s=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>cluster</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          takes a graph in DOT format as input, finds node
                          clusters, and then augments the graph with this
                          information
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"diffimg" name=3D"diffimg"></a><span clas=
s=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>diffimg</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          (needs <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://www.lib=
gd.org/">GD Library</a>) generates
                          an image where each pixel is the difference betwe=
en
                          the corresponding pixel in each of the two source
                          images
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"dijkstra" name=3D"dijkstra"></a><span cl=
ass=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>dijkstra</strong></span></span=
>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          reads a stream of graphs and for each computes th=
e
                          distance of every node from sourcenode
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"dot" name=3D"dot"></a><span class=3D"ter=
m"><span class=3D"command"><strong>dot</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          draws directed graphs. It works well on DAGs and
                          other graphs that can be drawn as hierarchies. It
                          reads attributed graph files and writes drawings.
                          By default, the output format dot is the input fi=
le
                          with layout coordinates appended
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"dot2gxl" name=3D"dot2gxl"></a><span clas=
s=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>dot2gxl</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          converts between graphs represented in GXL and in
                          the DOT language. Unless a conversion type is
                          specified using a flag, <span class=3D"command"><=
strong>gxl2dot</strong></span> will
                          deduce the type of conversion from the suffix of
                          the input file, a <code class=3D"filename">.dot</=
code> suffix causing a conversion
                          from DOT to GXL, and a <code class=3D"filename">.=
gxl</code> suffix causing a conversion
                          from GXL to DOT
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"edgepaint" name=3D"edgepaint"></a><span =
class=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>edgepaint</strong></span></s=
pan>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          performs edge coloring to disambiguate crossing
                          edges
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"fdp" name=3D"fdp"></a><span class=3D"ter=
m"><span class=3D"command"><strong>fdp</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          draws undirected graphs using a <span class=3D"qu=
ote">=E2=80=9C<span class=3D"quote">spring</span>=E2=80=9D</span>
                          model. It relies on a force-directed approach in
                          the spirit of Fruchterman and Reingold
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"gc-graphviz" name=3D"gc-graphviz"></a><s=
pan class=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>gc</strong></span></span=
>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          is a graph analogue to <span class=3D"command"><s=
trong>wc</strong></span> in that it
                          prints to standard output the number of nodes,
                          edges, connected components or clusters contained
                          in the input files. It also prints a total count
                          for all graphs if more than one graph is given
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"gml2gv" name=3D"gml2gv"></a><span class=
=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>gml2gv</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          converts a graph specified in the GML format to a
                          graph in the GV (formerly DOT) format
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"graphml2gv" name=3D"graphml2gv"></a><spa=
n class=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>graphml2gv</strong></span>=
</span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          converts a graph specified in the GRAPHML format =
to
                          a graph in the GV (formerly DOT) format
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"gv2gml" name=3D"gv2gml"></a><span class=
=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>gv2gml</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          converts a graph specified in the GV format to a
                          graph in the GML format
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"gv2gxl" name=3D"gv2gxl"></a><span class=
=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>gv2gxl</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          converts a graph specified in the GV format to a
                          graph in the GXL format
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"gvcolor" name=3D"gvcolor"></a><span clas=
s=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>gvcolor</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          is a filter that sets node colors from initial se=
ed
                          values. Colors flow along edges from tail to head=
,
                          and are averaged (as HSB vectors) at nodes
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"gvedit" name=3D"gvedit"></a><span class=
=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>gvedit</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          provides a simple graph editor and viewer. It
                          allows many graphs to be viewed at the same time.
                          The text of each graph is displayed in its own te=
xt
                          window
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"gvgen" name=3D"gvgen"></a><span class=3D=
"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>gvgen</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          generates a variety of simple, regularly-structur=
ed
                          abstract graphs
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"gvmap" name=3D"gvmap"></a><span class=3D=
"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>gvmap</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          takes as input a graph in DOT format, finds node
                          clusters and produces a rendering of the graph as=
 a
                          geographic-style map, with clusters highlighted, =
in
                          xdot format
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"gvmap.sh" name=3D"gvmap.sh"></a><span cl=
ass=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>gvmap.sh</strong></span></span=
>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          is a pipeline for running gvmap
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"gvpack" name=3D"gvpack"></a><span class=
=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>gvpack</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          reads in a stream of graphs, combines the graphs
                          into a single layout, and produces a single graph
                          serving as the union of the input graphs
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"gvpr" name=3D"gvpr"></a><span class=3D"t=
erm"><span class=3D"command"><strong>gvpr</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          is a graph stream editor inspired by <span class=
=3D"command"><strong>awk</strong></span>. It copies
                          input graphs to its output, possibly transforming
                          their structure and attributes, creating new
                          graphs, or printing arbitrary information
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"gxl2dot" name=3D"gxl2dot"></a><span clas=
s=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>gxl2dot</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          converts between graphs represented in GXL and in
                          the DOT language. Unless a conversion type is
                          specified using a flag, <span class=3D"command"><=
strong>gxl2dot</strong></span> will
                          deduce the type of conversion from the suffix of
                          the input file, a <code class=3D"filename">.dot</=
code> suffix causing a conversion
                          from DOT to GXL, and a <code class=3D"filename">.=
gxl</code> suffix causing a conversion
                          from GXL to DOT
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"gxl2gv" name=3D"gxl2gv"></a><span class=
=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>gxl2gv</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          converts between graphs represented in GXL and in
                          the GV language
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"mm2gv" name=3D"mm2gv"></a><span class=3D=
"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>mm2gv</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          converts a sparse matrix of the Matrix Market
                          format to a graph in the GV (formerly DOT) format
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"neato" name=3D"neato"></a><span class=3D=
"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>neato</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          draws undirected graphs using <span class=3D"quot=
e">=E2=80=9C<span class=3D"quote">spring</span>=E2=80=9D</span>
                          models. Input files must be formatted in the
                          <span class=3D"command"><strong>dot</strong></spa=
n>
                          attributed graph language. By default, the output
                          of <span class=3D"command"><strong>neato</strong>=
</span> is the
                          input graph with layout coordinates appended
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"nop" name=3D"nop"></a><span class=3D"ter=
m"><span class=3D"command"><strong>nop</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          reads a stream of graphs and prints each in
                          pretty-printed (canonical) format on stdout. If n=
o
                          files are given, it reads from stdin
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"osage" name=3D"osage"></a><span class=3D=
"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>osage</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          draws clustered graphs. It takes any graph in DOT
                          format as input
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"patchwork" name=3D"patchwork"></a><span =
class=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>patchwork</strong></span></s=
pan>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          draws clustered graphs using a squarified treemap
                          layout. It takes any graph in DOT format as input
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"prune" name=3D"prune"></a><span class=3D=
"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>prune</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          reads directed graphs in the same format used by
                          <span class=3D"command"><strong>dot</strong></spa=
n>
                          and removes subgraphs rooted at nodes specified o=
n
                          the command line via options
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"sccmap" name=3D"sccmap"></a><span class=
=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>sccmap</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          decomposes digraphs into strongly connected
                          components and an auxiliary map of the relationsh=
ip
                          between components. In this map, each component i=
s
                          collapsed into a node. The resulting graphs are
                          printed to stdout
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"sfdp" name=3D"sfdp"></a><span class=3D"t=
erm"><span class=3D"command"><strong>sfdp</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          draws undirected graphs using the <span class=3D"=
quote">=E2=80=9C<span class=3D"quote">spring</span>=E2=80=9D</span>
                          model, but it uses a multi-scale approach to
                          produce layouts of large graphs in a reasonably
                          short time
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"tred" name=3D"tred"></a><span class=3D"t=
erm"><span class=3D"command"><strong>tred</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          computes the transitive reduction of directed
                          graphs, and prints the resulting graphs to standa=
rd
                          output. This removes edges implied by transitivit=
y.
                          Nodes and subgraphs are not otherwise affected
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"twopi" name=3D"twopi"></a><span class=3D=
"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>twopi</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          draws graphs using a radial layout. Basically, on=
e
                          node is chosen as the center and put at the origi=
n.
                          The remaining nodes are placed on a sequence of
                          concentric circles centered about the origin, eac=
h
                          a fixed radial distance from the previous circle
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"unflatten" name=3D"unflatten"></a><span =
class=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>unflatten</strong></span></s=
pan>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          is a preprocessor to <span class=3D"command"><str=
ong>dot</strong></span> that is used
                          to improve the aspect ratio of graphs having many
                          leaves or disconnected nodes. The usual layout fo=
r
                          such a graph is generally very wide or tall
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"vimdot" name=3D"vimdot"></a><span class=
=3D"term"><span class=3D"command"><strong>vimdot</strong></span></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          is a simple script which launches the <span class=
=3D"command"><strong>gvim</strong></span> or
                          <span class=3D"command"><strong>vim</strong></spa=
n>
                          editor along with a GUI window showing the
                          <span class=3D"command"><strong>dot</strong></spa=
n>
                          output of the edited file
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"libcdt" name=3D"libcdt"></a><span class=
=3D"term"><code class=3D"filename">libcdt.so</code></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          manages run-time dictionaries using standard
                          container data types: unordered set/multiset,
                          ordered set/multiset, list, stack, and queue
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"libcgraph" name=3D"libcgraph"></a><span =
class=3D"term"><code class=3D"filename">libcgraph.so</code></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          supports graph programming by maintaining graphs =
in
                          memory and reading and writing graph files. Graph=
s
                          are composed of nodes, edges, and nested subgraph=
s
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"libgvc" name=3D"libgvc"></a><span class=
=3D"term"><code class=3D"filename">libgvc.so</code></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          provides a context for applications wishing to
                          manipulate and render graphs. It provides command
                          line parsing interfaces, common rendering code, a=
nd
                          a plugin mechanism for renderers
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"libpathplan" name=3D"libpathplan"></a><s=
pan class=3D"term"><code class=3D"filename">libpathplan.so</code></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          contains functions to find the shortest path
                          between two points in a simple polygon
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          <a id=3D"libxdot" name=3D"libxdot"></a><span clas=
s=3D"term"><code class=3D"filename">libxdot.so</code></span>
                        </p>
                      </td>
                      <td>
                        <p>
                          provides support for parsing and deparsing
                          graphical operations specified by the xdot langua=
ge
                        </p>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class=3D"titlepage">
            <div>
              <div>
                <h2 class=3D"title">
                  <a id=3D"gtk-doc" name=3D"gtk-doc"></a>GTK-Doc-1.34.0
                </h2>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class=3D"sect1" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
            <div class=3D"package" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Introduction to GTK-Doc
              </h3>
              <p>
                The <span class=3D"application">GTK-Doc</span> package cont=
ains
                a code documenter. This is useful for extracting specially
                formatted comments from the code to create API documentatio=
n.
                This package is <span class=3D"emphasis"><em>optional</em><=
/span>; if it is not installed,
                packages will not build the documentation. This does not me=
an
                that you will not have any documentation. If <span class=3D=
"application">GTK-Doc</span> is not available, the install
                process will copy any pre-built documentation to your syste=
m.
              </p>
              <p>
                This package is known to build and work properly using an L=
FS
                12.2 platform.
              </p>
              <h4>
                <a id=3D"idm140470522321840" name=3D"idm140470522321840"></=
a>Package Information
              </h4>
              <div class=3D"itemizedlist">
                <ul class=3D"compact">
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download (HTTP): <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://d=
ownload.gnome.org/sources/gtk-doc/1.34/gtk-doc-1.34.0.tar.xz">
                      https://download.gnome.org/sources/gtk-doc/1.34/gtk-d=
oc-1.34.0.tar.xz</a>
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li class=3D"listitem">
                    <p>
                      Download MD5 sum: f0e7385ba25eddb6ce0953e8cf63d1bf
