5328"></a>Configuration Information
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  The files in <code class=3D"filename">/etc/unixODBC</code=
>
                  are meant to be configured by the system administrator (o=
r
                  the ODBC site administrator if appropriate privileges are
                  granted to <code class=3D"filename">/etc/unixODBC</code>)=
.
                  These files are not meant to be directly edited. The ODBC
                  installer library is responsible for reading and writing
                  the <span class=3D"application">unixODBC</span> config fi=
les.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Unfortunately, there are not many <span class=3D"command"=
><strong>man</strong></span>, or any <span class=3D"command"><strong>info</=
strong></span> pages for the
                  various programs available in the <span class=3D"applicat=
ion">unixODBC</span> package. Along with the
                  information in the <span class=3D"quote">=E2=80=9C<span c=
lass=3D"quote">Short Descriptions</span>=E2=80=9D</span> below and the
                  documentation installed in <code class=3D"filename">/usr/=
share/doc/unixODBC-2.3.12</code>, there are
                  many <code class=3D"filename">README</code> files through=
out
                  the source tree where the use and functionality of the
                  programs can be found. Additionally, you can use the
                  parameter <code class=3D"option">-?</code> for syntax and
                  usage information. Lastly, the <span class=3D"application=
">unixODBC</span> web site at <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"http://www.unixodb=
c.org/">http://www.unixodbc.org/</a> has
                  very good information.
                </p>
