            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Installation of acpid
              </h3>
              <p>
                Install <span class=3D"application">acpid</span> by running=
 the
                following commands:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">./configure -=
-prefix=3D/usr \
            --docdir=3D/usr/share/doc/acpid-2.0.34 &amp;&amp;
make</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                This package does not come with a test suite.
              </p>
              <p>
                Now, as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make install      =
                   &amp;&amp;
install -v -m755 -d /etc/acpi/events &amp;&amp;
cp -r samples /usr/share/doc/acpid-2.0.34</kbd></pre>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"configuration" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Configuring acpid
              </h3>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"application">acpid</span> is configured by u=
ser
                defined events. Place event files under <code class=3D"file=
name">/etc/acpi/events</code> directory. If an event
                occurs, <span class=3D"command"><strong>acpid</strong></spa=
n>
                recurses through the event files in order to see if the reg=
ex
                defined after "event" matches. If they do, action is
                executed.
              </p>
              <p>
                The following brief example will suspend the system when th=
e
                laptop lid is closed <span class=3D"phrase">(it requires
                <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/=
blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#pm-utils" title=3D"=
pm-utils-1.4.1">pm-utils-1.4.1</a>)</span>. The example also
                disables the default handling of the lid close event by
                <span class=3D"phrase"><a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://ww=
w.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.h=
tml#elogind" title=3D"elogind-255.5">elogind-255.5</a></span> when the syst=
em is
                on battery and not connected to any external monitor, in
                order to avoid a conflict:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt; /etc=
/acpi/events/lid &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal">event=3Dbutton/lid
action=3D/etc/acpi/lid.sh</code>
EOF

cat &gt; /etc/acpi/lid.sh &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal">#!/bin/sh
/bin/grep -q open /proc/acpi/button/lid/LID/state &amp;&amp; exit 0
/usr/sbin/pm-suspend</code>
EOF
chmod +x /etc/acpi/lid.sh

mkdir -pv /etc/elogind/logind.conf.d
echo <code class=3D"literal">HandleLidSwitch=3Dignore</code> &gt; /etc/elog=
ind/logind.conf.d/acpi.conf</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                Unfortunately, not every computer labels ACPI events in the
                same way (for example, the lid may be recognized as
                <code class=3D"filename">LID0</code> instead of <code class=
=3D"filename">LID</code>). To determine how your buttons are
                recognized, use the <span class=3D"command"><strong>acpi_li=
sten</strong></span> tool. Also,
                look in the <code class=3D"filename">samples</code> directo=
ry
                under <code class=3D"filename">/usr/share/doc/acpid-2.0.34<=
/code> for more
                examples.
              </p>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"acpid-init" name=3D"acpid-init"></a><span =
class=3D"phrase">Boot Script</span>
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  To automatically start <span class=3D"command"><strong>ac=
pid</strong></span> when the system is
                  rebooted, install the <code class=3D"filename">/etc/rc.d/=
init.d/acpid</code> boot script from
                  the <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratc=
h.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#bootscripts" =
title=3D"BLFS Boot Scripts">blfs-bootscripts-20240416</a> package.
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make install-acp=
id</kbd></pre>
              </div>
            </div>
