            <div class=3D"kernel" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                <a id=3D"elogind-kernel" name=3D"elogind-kernel"></a>Kernel
                Configuration
              </h3>
              <p>
                Enable the following options in the kernel configuration an=
d
                recompile the kernel if necessary:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"screen"><span class=3D"blue">F</span>ile system=
s ---&gt;
  [*] <span class=3D"blue">I</span>notify support for userspace            =
                   [INOTIFY_USER]
  <span class=3D"blue">P</span>seudo filesystems ---&gt;
    [*] <span class=3D"blue">T</span>mpfs virtual memory file system suppor=
t (former shm fs)         [TMPFS]
    [*]   <span class=3D"blue">T</span>mpfs POSIX Access Control Lists     =
                [TMPFS_POSIX_ACL]</pre>
              <p>
                In addition, some tests need the userspace cryptographic
                kernel API, which is enabled with:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"screen">-*- <span class=3D"blue">C</span>ryptog=
raphic API ---&gt;                                              [CRYPTO]
  <span class=3D"blue">C</span>rypto core or helper ---&gt;
    &lt;*/M&gt; <span class=3D"blue">U</span>serspace cryptographic algorit=
hm configuration          [CRYPTO_USER]
  <span class=3D"blue">U</span>serspace interface ---&gt;
    &lt;*/M&gt; H<span class=3D"blue">a</span>sh algorithms                =
                 [CRYPTO_USER_API_HASH]</pre>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Installation of elogind
              </h3>
              <p>
                Install <span class=3D"application">elogind</span> by runni=
ng
                the following commands:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">mkdir build &=
amp;&amp;
cd    build &amp;&amp;

meson setup ..                               \
      --prefix=3D/usr                          \
      --buildtype=3Drelease                    \
      -D man=3Dauto                            \
      -D docdir=3D/usr/share/doc/elogind-255.5 \
      -D cgroup-controller=3Delogind           \
      -D dev-kvm-mode=3D0660                   \
      -D dbuspolicydir=3D/etc/dbus-1/system.d &amp;&amp;
ninja</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                To test the results, issue: <span class=3D"command"><strong=
>ninja test</strong></span>. A few tests are
                skipped if not run with <code class=3D"systemitem">root</co=
de>
                privileges. Three tests, <code class=3D"filename">test-logi=
n</code>, <code class=3D"filename">dbus-docs-fresh</code>, and <code class=
=3D"filename">check-version-history</code>, are known to fail.
              </p>
              <p>
                Now, as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">ninja install     =
                                      &amp;&amp;
ln -sfv  libelogind.pc /usr/lib/pkgconfig/libsystemd.pc &amp;&amp;
ln -sfvn elogind /usr/include/systemd</kbd></pre>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"commands" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Command Explanations
              </h3>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-D
                docdir=3D/usr/share/doc/elogind-255.5</code></em>: This is
                needed to install documentation in a versioned directory.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-D
                cgroup-controller=3Delogind</code></em>: This switch is
                necessary to build this package when the kernel is not buil=
t
                with <code class=3D"option">CONFIG_CGROUPS</code> enabled. =
Note
                that <span class=3D"application">elogind</span> strictly ne=
eds
                a kernel with <code class=3D"option">CONFIG_CGROUPS</code>
                enabled at runtime, but this switch will allow building the
                package first.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-D
                dbuspolicydir=3D/etc/dbus-1/system.d</code></em>: This swit=
ch
                sets the location of the <span class=3D"application">D-Bus<=
/span> policy directory.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-D dev-kvm-mode=3D0660</code>=
</em>:
                The LFS udev rule sets the mode of <code class=3D"filename"=
>/dev/kvm</code> to 0660. This option ensures the
                elogind udev rules consistent with the LFS configuration.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-D man=3Dauto</code></em>: Th=
e
                default value of this switch is <span class=3D"emphasis"><e=
m>false</em></span>. Setting it to <span class=3D"emphasis"><em>auto</em></=
span> allows building and
                installing the man pages if the recommended dependencies ar=
e
                installed.
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">-D html=3Dauto</code>: The default v=
alue
                of this switch is <span class=3D"emphasis"><em>false</em></=
span>. Setting it to <span class=3D"emphasis"><em>auto</em></span> allows b=
uilding and
                installing the html documentation if the recommended
                dependencies are installed.
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">-D
                default-kill-user-processes=3Dfalse</code>: Determines whet=
her
                the processes of a user should be killed when the user logs
                out. The default is <span class=3D"emphasis"><em>true</em><=
/span>, but this defeats the
                traditional use of <span class=3D"command"><strong>screen</=
strong></span> or <span class=3D"command"><strong>tmux</strong></span>. Thi=
s can also be
                changed in the configuration file (see below).
              </p>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>ln -s ...</strong></span>:
                These commands install symlinks so that software packages c=
an
                find the systemd-compatible library and headers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"configuration" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Configuring elogind
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"elogind-config" name=3D"elogind-config"></=
a>Config File
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  <code class=3D"filename">/etc/elogind/logind.conf</code>
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"idm140470520168672" name=3D"idm14047052016=
8672"></a>Configuration Information
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  The installed file <code class=3D"filename">/etc/elogind/=
logind.conf</code> contains all the
                  possible options with their defaults, commented out. You
                  may wish to disable automatically killing user processes
                  when the user logs out, by running, as the <code class=3D=
"systemitem">root</code> user:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">sed -e '/\[Login=
\]/a KillUserProcesses=3Dno' \
    -i /etc/elogind/logind.conf</kbd></pre>
                <p>
                  Each user will need to register a user session using
                  <span class=3D"application">Linux-PAM</span> at login. Th=
e
                  <code class=3D"filename">/etc/pam.d/system-session</code>
                  file needs to be modified and a new file must be created =
in
                  order for <span class=3D"command"><strong>elogind</strong=
></span> to work
                  correctly. Run the following commands as the <code class=
=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt;&gt; /et=
c/pam.d/system-session &lt;&lt; "EOF" &amp;&amp;
<code class=3D"literal"># Begin elogind addition

session  required    pam_loginuid.so
session  optional    pam_elogind.so

# End elogind addition</code>
EOF
cat &gt; /etc/pam.d/elogind-user &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal"># Begin /etc/pam.d/elogind-user

account  required    pam_access.so
account  include     system-account

session  required    pam_env.so
session  required    pam_limits.so
session  required    pam_unix.so
session  required    pam_loginuid.so
session  optional    pam_keyinit.so force revoke
session  optional    pam_elogind.so

auth     required    pam_deny.so
password required    pam_deny.so

# End /etc/pam.d/elogind-user</code>
EOF</kbd></pre>
                <div class=3D"admon note">
                  <h3>
                    Note
                  </h3>
                  <p>
                    After completion of <span class=3D"application">elogind=
</span>, you should check that it
                    functions properly. First ensure that <span class=3D"ap=
plication">dbus</span> is running. It may be easiest
                    to do this by rebooting the system. After logging in
                    again, run the command <span class=3D"command"><strong>=
loginctl</strong></span>. The result
                    should indicate that a SESSION and a SEAT have been
                    created.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"content" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
