tives
                </h2>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class=3D"sect1" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
            <p>
              The presence or absence of the <span class=3D"command"><stron=
g>which</strong></span> program in the main LFS
              book is probably one of the most contentious issues on the
              mailing lists. It has resulted in at least one flame war in t=
he
              past. To hopefully put an end to this once and for all,
              presented here are two options for equipping your system with
              <span class=3D"command"><strong>which</strong></span>. The
              question of which <span class=3D"quote">=E2=80=9C<span class=
=3D"quote"><span class=3D"command"><strong>which</strong></span></span>=E2=
=80=9D</span> is for
              you to decide.
            </p>
            <div class=3D"package" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Introduction to GNU Which
              </h3>
              <p>
                The first option is to install the actual GNU <span class=
=3D"application">which</span> package.
              </p>
              <p>
                This package is known to build and work properly using an L=
FS
                12.2 platform.
              </p>
              <h4>
