            <div class=3D"sect2" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                <a id=3D"perl-alternatives" name=3D"perl-alternatives"></a>=
CPAN
                automated install of perl modules
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"perl-auto-install" name=3D"perl-auto-insta=
ll"></a> Automatic Installation of
                        Perl Modules.
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  There is an alternate way of installing the modules using
                  the <span class=3D"command"><strong>cpan</strong></span>
                  shell <span class=3D"command"><strong>install</strong></s=
pan>
                  command. The command automatically downloads the latest
                  source from the CPAN archive for the module and any missi=
ng
                  prerequisite modules listed by upstream. Then for each
                  module it extracts it, runs the compilation, the tests an=
d
                  installs it.
                </p>
                <p>
                  You still need to install any non-perl dependencies befor=
e
                  running the automated installation method. You may wish t=
o
                  clean out the <code class=3D"filename">build/</code>
                  directory after installing, to free up the space. If any
                  post-install actions such as creating a symlink are
                  mentioned, you should also do those.
                </p>
                <p>
                  The first time you run <span class=3D"command"><strong>cp=
an</strong></span>, you'll be prompted
                  to enter some information regarding download locations an=
d
                  methods. This information is retained in files located in
                  <code class=3D"filename">~/.cpan</code>.
                </p>
                <p>
                  In particular, you may wish to configure it so that
                  <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.or=
g/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#sudo" title=3D"Su=
do-1.9.15p5">Sudo-1.9.15p5</a> is used for the installs,
                  allowing you to build and test as a regular user. The
                  following examples have not used that approach.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Start the <span class=3D"command"><strong>cpan</strong></=
span> shell by issuing
                  '<span class=3D"command"><strong>cpan</strong></span>' as=
 the
                  <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user. Any module m=
ay
                  now be installed from the <span class=3D"underlined"><cod=
e class=3D"prompt">cpan&gt;&nbsp;</code></span> prompt with the
                  command:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">install <em clas=
s=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;Module::Name&gt;</code></em></kbd></pre>
                <p>
                  For additional commands and help, issue '<span class=3D"c=
ommand"><strong>help</strong></span>' from the
                  <span class=3D"underlined"><code class=3D"prompt">cpan&gt=
;&nbsp;</code></span> prompt.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Alternatively, for scripted or non-interactive
                  installations, use the following syntax as the <code clas=
s=3D"systemitem">root</code> user to install one or more
                  modules:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">cpan -i <em clas=
s=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;Module1::Name&gt; &lt;Module2::Name&gt;</code><=
/em></kbd></pre>
                <p>
                  Review the <code class=3D"filename">cpan.1</code> man pag=
e
                  for additional parameters you can pass to <span class=3D"=
command"><strong>cpan</strong></span> on the command line.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class=3D"titlepage">
            <div>
              <div>
                <h2 class=3D"title">
                  <a id=3D"perl-deps" name=3D"perl-deps"></a>Perl Module
                  Dependencies
                </h2>
