            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Installation of OpenJDK
              </h3>
              <p>
                If you have downloaded the optional test harness, unpack it
                now:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">tar -xf ../jt=
reg-7.3.1+1.tar.gz</kbd></pre>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  Before proceeding, you should ensure that your environmen=
t
                  <code class=3D"envar">PATH</code> variable contains the
                  location of the Java compiler used for bootstrapping
                  <span class=3D"application">OpenJDK</span>. This is the o=
nly
                  requirement for the environment. Modern Java installation=
s
                  do not need <code class=3D"envar">JAVA_HOME</code> and
                  <code class=3D"envar">CLASSPATH</code> is not used here.
                  Furthermore, OpenJDK developers recommend unsetting
                  <code class=3D"envar">JAVA_HOME</code>.
                </p>
                <p>
                  The build system does not allow the <code class=3D"option=
">-j</code> switch in <code class=3D"envar">MAKEFLAGS</code>. See the comma=
nd explanation for
                  <code class=3D"option">--with-jobs=3D</code> for more
                  information on customizing parallelization.
                </p>
              </div>
              <p>
                Configure and build the package with the following commands=
:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">unset JAVA_HO=
ME                             &amp;&amp;
bash configure --enable-unlimited-crypto    \
               --disable-warnings-as-errors \
               --with-stdc++lib=3Ddynamic     \
               --with-giflib=3Dsystem         \
               --with-harfbuzz=3Dsystem       \
               --with-jtreg=3D$PWD/jtreg      \
               --with-lcms=3Dsystem           \
               --with-libjpeg=3Dsystem        \
               --with-libpng=3Dsystem         \
               --with-zlib=3Dsystem           \
               --with-version-build=3D"9"     \
               --with-version-pre=3D""        \
               --with-version-opt=3D""        \
               --with-cacerts-file=3D/etc/pki/tls/java/cacerts &amp;&amp;
make images</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                To test the results, you will need to execute the jtreg
                program. You can set the number of concurrent tests by addi=
ng
                the <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-conc:<em class=3D"replac=
eable"><code>&lt;X&gt;</code></em></code></em> value
                in the below command (tests will run sequentially otherwise=
):
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">export JT_JAV=
A=3D$(echo $PWD/build/*/jdk) &amp;&amp;
jtreg/bin/jtreg -jdk:$JT_JAVA -automatic -ignore:quiet -v1 \
    test/jdk:tier1 test/langtools:tier1 &amp;&amp;
unset JT_JAVA</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                For more control over the test suite, review the
                documentation available in <code class=3D"filename">jtreg/d=
oc/jtreg/usage.txt</code>. To review the
                results, see the files <code class=3D"filename">JTreport/te=
st_{jdk,langtools}/text/stats.txt</code>
                and <code class=3D"filename">JTreport/test_{jdk,langtools}/=
text/summary.txt</code>.
                You should expect to see around 60 failures and 10 errors.
              </p>
              <p>
                Install the package with the following commands as the
                <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">install -vdm755 /o=
pt/jdk-22.0.2+9             &amp;&amp;
cp -Rv build/*/images/jdk/* /opt/jdk-22.0.2+9 &amp;&amp;
chown -R root:root /opt/jdk-22.0.2+9          &amp;&amp;
for s in 16 24 32 48; do
  install -vDm644 src/java.desktop/unix/classes/sun/awt/X11/java-icon${s}.p=
ng \
                  /usr/share/icons/hicolor/${s}x${s}/apps/java.png
done</kbd></pre>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  If you only wish to install the Java Runtime Environment,
                  you can substitute <code class=3D"filename">build/*/image=
s/jre</code> in the above
                  <span class=3D"command"><strong>cp</strong></span> comman=
d.
                </p>
              </div>
              <p>
                There are now two <span class=3D"application">OpenJDK</span=
>
                SDKs installed in <code class=3D"filename">/opt</code>. You
                should decide on which one you would like to use as the
                default. Normally, you would opt for the just installed
                <span class=3D"application">OpenJDK</span>. If so, do the
                following as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> use=
r:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">ln -v -nsf jdk-22.=
0.2+9 /opt/jdk</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                If desired, you can create .desktop files to add entries in
                the menu for <span class=3D"command"><strong>java</strong><=
/span> and <span class=3D"command"><strong>jconsole</strong></span>. The ne=
eded icons
                have already been installed. As the <code class=3D"systemit=
em">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">mkdir -pv /usr/sha=
re/applications &amp;&amp;

cat &gt; /usr/share/applications/openjdk-java.desktop &lt;&lt; "EOF" &amp;&=
amp;
<code class=3D"literal">[Desktop Entry]
Name=3DOpenJDK Java 22.0.2 Runtime
Comment=3DOpenJDK Java 22.0.2 Runtime
Exec=3D/opt/jdk/bin/java -jar
Terminal=3Dfalse
Type=3DApplication
Icon=3Djava
MimeType=3Dapplication/x-java-archive;application/java-archive;application/=
x-jar;
NoDisplay=3Dtrue</code>
EOF
cat &gt; /usr/share/applications/openjdk-jconsole.desktop &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal">[Desktop Entry]
Name=3DOpenJDK Java 22.0.2 Console
Comment=3DOpenJDK Java 22.0.2 Console
Keywords=3Djava;console;monitoring
Exec=3D/opt/jdk/bin/jconsole
Terminal=3Dfalse
Type=3DApplication
Icon=3Djava
Categories=3DApplication;System;</code>
EOF</kbd></pre>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"commands" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Command Explanations
              </h3>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>bash
                configure...</strong></span>: the top level <code class=3D"=
filename">configure</code> is a wrapper around the autotools
                one. It is not executable and must be run through
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>bash</strong></span>.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--enable-unlimited-crypto</co=
de></em>:
                Because of limitations on the usage of cryptography in some
                countries, there is the possibility to limit the size of
                encryption keys and the use of some algorithms in a policy
                file. This switch allows to ship a policy file with no
                restriction. It is the responsibility of the user to ensure
                proper adherence to the law.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--disable-warnings-as-errors<=
/code></em>:
                This switch disables use of <code class=3D"envar">-Werror</=
code> in the build.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-stdc++lib=3Ddynamic</c=
ode></em>: This
                switch forces the build system to link to <code class=3D"fi=
lename">libstdc++.so</code> (dynamic) instead of
                <code class=3D"filename">libstdc++.a</code> (static).
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">--with-jobs=3D<em class=3D"replaceab=
le"><code>&lt;X&gt;</code></em></code>: The
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-j</code></em> passed to make
                does not work with make as invoked here. By default, the
                build system will use the number of CPUs - 1.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-jtreg=3D$PWD/jtreg</co=
de></em>: This
                switch tells configure where to find jtreg. Omit if you hav=
e
                not downloaded the optional test suite.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-{giflib,harfbuzz,lcms,=
libjpeg,libpng,zlib}=3Dsystem</code></em>:
                These switches force the build system to use the system
                libraries instead of the bundled versions.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-version-build</code></=
em>:
                Currently, the build system does not include the build numb=
er
                in the version string. It has to be specified here.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-version-pre</code></em=
>:
                This switch allows you to prefix the version string with a
                custom string.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-version-opt</code></em=
>:
                This switch allows you to add an optional build description
                to the version string.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-cacerts-file=3D/etc/pk=
i/tls/java/cacerts</code></em>:
                Specifies where to find a <code class=3D"filename">cacerts<=
/code> file, <code class=3D"filename">/etc/pki/tls/java/</code> on a BLFS s=
ystem.
                Otherwise, an empty one is created. You can use the
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>/usr/sbin/make-ca
                --force</strong></span> command to generate it, once you ha=
ve
                installed the Java binaries.
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">--with-boot-jdk</code>: This switch
                provides the location of the temporary <span class=3D"appli=
cation">JDK</span>. It is normally not needed if
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>java</strong></span> is fou=
nd
                in the <code class=3D"envar">PATH</code>.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"configuration" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                <a id=3D"openjdk-config" name=3D"openjdk-config"></a>Config=
uring
                OpenJDK
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"idm140470507998288" name=3D"idm14047050799=
8288"></a>Configuration Information
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  Normally, the Java environment has been configured after
                  installing the binary version, and can be used with the
                  just built package as well. Review <a class=3D"xref" href=
=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-s=
ysv-nochunks.html#ojdk-conf" title=3D"Configuring the Java environment">Con=
figuring the Java
                  environment</a> in case you want to modify something.
                </p>
                <p>
                  To test if the man pages are correctly installed, issue
                  <span class=3D"command"><strong>source
                  /etc/profile</strong></span> and <span class=3D"command">=
<strong>man java</strong></span> to display the
                  respective man page.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"idm140470507994240" name=3D"idm14047050799=
4240"></a> Setting up the JRE
                        Certificate Authority Certificates (cacerts) file
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  If you have run the instructions on the <a class=3D"xref"=
 href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-1=
2.2-sysv-nochunks.html#make-ca" title=3D"make-ca-1.14">make-ca-1.14</a> pag=
e,
                  you only need to create a symlink in the default location
                  for the <code class=3D"filename">cacerts</code> file. As =
user
                  <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code>:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">ln -sfv /etc/pki=
/tls/java/cacerts /opt/jdk/lib/security/cacerts</kbd></pre>
                <p>
                  To check the installation, issue:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">cd /opt/jdk
bin/keytool -list -cacerts</kbd></pre>
                <p>
                  At the prompt <code class=3D"computeroutput">Enter keysto=
re
                  password:</code>, enter <strong class=3D"userinput"><code=
>changeit</code></strong> (the default) or
                  just press the <span class=3D"quote">=E2=80=9C<span class=
=3D"quote">Enter</span>=E2=80=9D</span> key. If the <code class=3D"filename=
">cacerts</code> file was installed correctly, you
                  will see a list of the certificates with related
                  information for each one. If not, you need to reinstall
                  them.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
