pcbind-1.2.7">rpcbind-1.2.7</a>
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"kernel" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                <a id=3D"nfs-utils-kernel" name=3D"nfs-utils-kernel"></a>Ke=
rnel
                Configuration
              </h3>
              <p>
                Enable the following options in the kernel configuration
                (choose client and/or server support as appropriate) and
                recompile the kernel if necessary:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"screen"><span class=3D"blue">F</span>ile system=
s ---&gt;
  [*] N<span class=3D"blue">e</span>twork File Systems ---&gt;             =
               [NETWORK_FILESYSTEMS]
    &lt;*/M&gt; N<span class=3D"blue">F</span>S client support             =
                               [NFS_FS]
    &lt;*/M&gt; N<span class=3D"blue">F</span>S server support             =
                                 [NFSD]</pre>
              <p>
                Select the appropriate sub-options that appear when the abo=
ve
                options are selected.
              </p>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  In BLFS we assume that nfs v3 will be used. If the
                  <span class=3D"emphasis"><em>server</em></span> offers nf=
s v4
                  (for linux, CONFIG_NFSD_V4) then auto-negotiation for v3
                  will fail and you will need to add <code class=3D"literal=
">nfsver=3D3</code> to the mount options. This also
                  applies if that option is enabled in the <span class=3D"e=
mphasis"><em>client's</em></span> kernel, for example in
                  a distro trying to mount from a BLFS v3 server.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Even if neither end of the connection supports nfs v4,
                  adding <code class=3D"literal">nfsver=3D3</code> is still
                  beneficial because it prevents an error message "NFS: bad
                  mount option value specified: minorversion=3D1" being log=
ged
                  on every mount.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                <a id=3D"nfs-utils-install" name=3D"nfs-utils-install"></a>=
Installation of NFS Utilities
              </h3>
              <p>
                Install <span class=3D"application">NFS Utilities</span> by
                running the following commands:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">./configure -=
-prefix=3D/usr          \
            --sysconfdir=3D/etc      \
            --sbindir=3D/usr/sbin    \
            --disable-nfsv4        \
            --disable-gss          \
            LIBS=3D"-lsqlite3 -levent_core" &amp;&amp;
make</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                Now, as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make install      =
                 &amp;&amp;
chmod u+w,go+r /usr/sbin/mount.nfs &amp;&amp;
chown nobody:nogroup /var/lib/nfs</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                The tests for this package require that the package be
                installed. In addition, the rpc.statd daemon must not be
                running and the tests need to be run as the <code class=3D"=
systemitem">root</code> user.
              </p>
              <p>
                To test the results, issue, as <code class=3D"systemitem">r=
oot</code>:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make check</kbd></=
pre>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"commands" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Command Explanations
              </h3>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--disable-gss</code></em>:
                Disables support for RPCSEC GSS (RPC Security).
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>LIBS=3D"-lsqlite3
                -levent_core"</code></em>: is required for the fsidd progra=
m.
              </p>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>chown nobody:nogroup
                /var/lib/nfs</strong></span>: The rpc.statd program uses th=
e
                ownership of this directory to set it's UID and GID. This
                command sets those to unprivileged entries.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"configuration" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Configuring NFS Utilities
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"nfs-utils-server-config" name=3D"nfs-utils=
-server-config"></a>Server Configuration
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  <code class=3D"filename">/etc/exports</code> contains the
                  exported directories on NFS servers. Refer to the
                  <code class=3D"filename">exports.5</code> manual page for=
 the
                  syntax of this file. Also refer to the "NFS HowTo"
                  available at <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://nfs.sourc=
eforge.net/nfs-howto/">https://nfs.sourceforge.net/nfs-howto/</a>
                  for information on how to configure the servers and clien=
ts
                  in a secure manner. For example, for sharing the
                  <code class=3D"filename">/home</code> directory over the
                  local network, the following line may be added:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt;&gt=
; /etc/exports &lt;&lt; EOF
<em class=3D"replaceable"><code>/home 192.168.0.0/24</code></em>(rw,subtree=
_check,anonuid=3D99,anongid=3D99)
EOF</kbd></pre>
                <div class=3D"admon note">
                  <h3>
                    Note
                  </h3>
                  <p>
                    Be sure to replace the directory, network address. and
                    prefix above to match your network. The only space in t=
he
                    line above should be between the directory and the
                    network address.
                  </p>
                </div>
                <div class=3D"sect4">
                  <div class=3D"titlepage">
                    <div>
                      <div>
                        <h5 class=3D"title">
                          <a id=3D"nfs-utils-server-init" name=3D"nfs-utils=
-server-init"></a><span class=3D"phrase">Boot Script</span>
                        </h5>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <p>
                    Install the <span class=3D"phrase"><code class=3D"filen=
ame">/etc/rc.d/init.d/nfs-server</code> init
                    script</span> included in the <a class=3D"xref" href=3D=
"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv=
-nochunks.html#bootscripts" title=3D"BLFS Boot Scripts">blfs-bootscripts-20=
240416</a> package
                    to start the server at boot.
                  </p>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make install-n=
fs-server</kbd></pre>
                  <p>
                    Now create the <code class=3D"filename">/etc/sysconfig/=
nfs-server</code> configuration
                    file:
                  </p>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt; /etc/=
sysconfig/nfs-server &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal">PORT=3D"2049"
PROCESSES=3D"8"
KILLDELAY=3D"10"</code>
EOF</kbd></pre>
                  <div class=3D"admon note">
                    <h3>
                      Note
                    </h3>
                    <p>
                      The above parameters may be optionally placed in
                      <code class=3D"filename">/etc/sysconfig/rc.site</code=
>.
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"nfs-utils-client-config" name=3D"nfs-utils=
-client-config"></a>Client Configuration
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  <code class=3D"filename">/etc/fstab</code> contains the
                  directories that are to be mounted on the client.
                  Alternately the partitions can be mounted by using the
                  <span class=3D"command"><strong>mount</strong></span> com=
mand
                  with the proper options. To mount the <code class=3D"file=
name">/home</code> and <code class=3D"filename">/usr</code> partitions, add=
 the following to the
                  <code class=3D"filename">/etc/fstab</code>:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command"><em class=
=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;server-name&gt;</code></em>:/home  /home nfs   r=
w,_netdev 0 0
<em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;server-name&gt;</code></em>:/usr   /usr=
  nfs   ro,_netdev 0 0</kbd></pre>
                <p>
                  The options which can be used are specified in <span clas=
s=3D"command"><strong>man 5 nfs</strong></span> . If both the
                  client and server are running recent versions of linux,
                  most of the options will be negotiated (but see the Note
                  above on nfsver=3D3). You can specify either <code class=
=3D"literal">rw</code> or <code class=3D"literal">ro</code>,
                  <code class=3D"literal">_netdev</code> if the filesystem =
is
                  to be automatically mounted at boot, or <code class=3D"li=
teral">noauto</code> (and perhaps <code class=3D"literal">user</code>) for =
other filesystems.
                </p>
                <p>
                  If the fileserver is not running a recent version of linu=
x,
                  you may need to specify other options.
                </p>
                <div class=3D"sect4">
                  <div class=3D"titlepage">
                    <div>
                      <div>
                        <h5 class=3D"title">
                          <a id=3D"nfs-utils-client-init" name=3D"nfs-utils=
-client-init"></a><span class=3D"phrase">Boot Script</span>
                        </h5>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <div class=3D"admon note">
                    <h3>
                      Note
                    </h3>
                    <p>
                      The following <span class=3D"phrase">boot script
                      is</span> not required if the nfs-server <span class=
=3D"phrase">script is</span> installed.
                    </p>
                  </div>
                  <p>
                    Install the <span class=3D"phrase"><code class=3D"filen=
ame">/etc/rc.d/init.d/nfs-client</code> init
                    script</span> included in the <a class=3D"xref" href=3D=
"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv=
-nochunks.html#bootscripts" title=3D"BLFS Boot Scripts">blfs-bootscripts-20=
240416</a> package
                    to start the client services at boot.
                  </p>
                  <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make install-n=
fs-client</kbd></pre>
                  <p>
                    To automatically mount <code class=3D"systemitem">nfs</=
code> filesystems, clients will also
                    need to install the <code class=3D"filename">netfs</cod=
e>
                    bootscript as described in <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"ht=
tps://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-no=
chunks.html#postlfs-config-netfs" title=3D"Configuring for Network Filesyst=
ems">Configuring for
                    Network Filesystems</a>.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
