            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Installation of PulseAudio
              </h3>
              <p>
                Install <span class=3D"application">PulseAudio</span> by
                running the following commands:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">mkdir build &=
amp;&amp;
cd    build &amp;&amp;

meson setup --prefix=3D/usr       \
            --buildtype=3Drelease \
            -D database=3Dgdbm    \
            -D doxygen=3Dfalse    \
            -D bluez5=3Ddisable   \
            ..                  &amp;&amp;
ninja</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                To test the results, issue: <span class=3D"command"><strong=
>ninja test</strong></span>. One test fails
                if the tests are not run as the root user, but this can be
                ignored.
              </p>
              <p>
                Now, as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">ninja install</kbd=
></pre>
              <p>
                Running PulseAudio as a system-wide daemon is possible but
                not recommended. See <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://www=
.freedesktop.org/wiki/Software/PulseAudio/Documentation/User/SystemWide/">
                https://www.freedesktop.org/wiki/Software/PulseAudio/Docume=
ntation/User/SystemWide/</a>
                for more information. While still as the <code class=3D"sys=
temitem">root</code> user, remove the <span class=3D"application">D-Bus</sp=
an> configuration file for the system
                wide daemon to avoid creating unnecessary system users and
                groups:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">rm /usr/share/dbus=
-1/system.d/pulseaudio-system.conf</kbd></pre>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"commands" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Command Explanations
              </h3>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--buildtype=3Drelease</code><=
/em>:
                Specify a buildtype suitable for stable releases of the
                package, as the default may produce unoptimized binaries.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-D bluez5=3Ddisabled</code></=
em>:
                This switch prevents a runtime error if <a class=3D"xref" h=
ref=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.=
2-sysv-nochunks.html#dbus" title=3D"dbus-1.14.10">dbus-1.14.10</a> and <a c=
lass=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stabl=
e/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#sbc" title=3D"SBC-2.0">SBC-2.0</a> are =
both
                installed but <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfr=
omscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#bluez=
" title=3D"BlueZ-5.77">BlueZ-5.77</a> is not installed. Remove this if
                you have installed all three packages.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>-D doxygen=3Dfalse</code></em=
>:
                This allows the package to compile if <a class=3D"xref" hre=
f=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-=
sysv-nochunks.html#doxygen" title=3D"Doxygen-1.12.0">Doxygen-1.12.0</a> is =
not
                installed. Remove this if you have installed this and wish =
to
                build the documentation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"configuration" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Configuring PulseAudio
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"pulseaudio-config" name=3D"pulseaudio-conf=
ig"></a>Config Files
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  There are system wide configuration files: <code class=3D=
"filename">/etc/pulse/daemon.conf</code>, <code class=3D"filename">/etc/pul=
se/client.conf</code>, <code class=3D"filename">/etc/pulse/default.pa</code=
>, and user
                  configuration files with the same names in <code class=3D=
"filename">~/.config/pulse</code>. User configuration files
                  take precedence over system wide ones.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"idm140470480339040" name=3D"idm14047048033=
9040"></a>Configuration Information
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  You may have to configure the audio system. You can start
                  <span class=3D"application">pulseaudio</span> in command =
line
                  mode using <span class=3D"command"><strong>pulseaudio
                  -C</strong></span> and then list various information and
                  change settings. See <span class=3D"command"><strong>man
                  pulse-cli-syntax</strong></span>.
                </p>
                <p>
                  If <span class=3D"application">pulseaudio</span> was work=
ing
                  but you no-longer have sound, after checking for hardware
                  issues (speakers or headphones not connected, external
                  amplifier not connected) you may need to fix it.
                </p>
                <p>
                  One suggestion is to close the application, such as
                  <span class=3D"application">firefox</span> where sound ha=
s
                  stopped working, then run: <span class=3D"command"><stron=
g>pactl list short sinks</strong></span>
                  followed by: <span class=3D"command"><strong>pacmd
                  set-default-sink &lt;sink #&gt;</strong></span> and then
                  restart the application.
                </p>
                <p>
                  If that does not work, a more drastic approach often work=
s.
                  After closing the application, close pulseaudio, either
                  using <span class=3D"command"><strong>pulseaudio
                  --kill</strong></span> or, if that fails, <span class=3D"=
command"><strong>killall -KILL pulseaudio</strong></span>
                  and then <span class=3D"command"><strong>rm -rf
                  ~/.config/pulse/*</strong></span> (and perhaps also
                  <span class=3D"command"><strong>rm -rf
                  ~/.pulse/*</strong></span> if you have used a very old
                  version of pulse on this machine), then run <span class=
=3D"command"><strong>pulseaudio --verbose</strong></span> to
                  restart it. If the daemon starts, restart the application=
.
                  See <span class=3D"command"><strong>man
                  pulseaudio</strong></span> for more options.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
