=3D"cups-filters-2.0.1">cups-filters-2.0.1</a>
              </p>
              <h5>
                <a id=3D"idm140470478042768" name=3D"idm140470478042768"></=
a>Optional (Postinstall)
              </h5>
              <p class=3D"optional">
                <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/=
blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#gutenprint" title=
=3D"Gutenprint-5.3.4">Gutenprint-5.3.4</a> and <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"h=
ttps://developers.hp.com/hp-linux-imaging-and-printing">hplip</a>
                (HP printers)
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"kernel" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                <a id=3D"cups-kernel" name=3D"cups-kernel"></a>Kernel
                Configuration
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  There used to be a conflict between the <span class=3D"ap=
plication">Cups</span> <span class=3D"application">libusb</span> backend an=
d the usblp kernel
                  driver. This is no longer the case and cups will work wit=
h
                  both of these enabled.
                </p>
              </div>
              <p>
                If you want to use the kernel usblp driver (for example, if
                you wish to use <span class=3D"application">escputil</span>
                from <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch=
.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#gutenprint" ti=
tle=3D"Gutenprint-5.3.4">Gutenprint-5.3.4</a>) enable the following
                options in your kernel configuration and recompile the
                kernel:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"screen"><span class=3D"blue">D</span>evice Driv=
ers ---&gt;
  [*] <span class=3D"blue">U</span>SB support ---&gt;                      =
                       [USB_SUPPORT]
    &lt;*/M&gt; <span class=3D"blue">U</span>SB Printer support            =
                          [USB_PRINTER]</pre>
              <p>
                If you have a parallel printer, enable the following option=
s
                in your kernel configuration and recompile the kernel:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"screen"><span class=3D"blue">D</span>evice Driv=
ers ---&gt;
  &lt;*/M&gt; <span class=3D"blue">P</span>arallel port support ---&gt;    =
                                 [PARPORT]
    &lt;*/M&gt; <span class=3D"blue">P</span>C-style hardware              =
                           [PARPORT_PC]
  <span class=3D"blue">C</span>haracter devices ---&gt;
    &lt;*/M&gt; <span class=3D"blue">P</span>arallel printer support       =
                              [PRINTER]</pre>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Installation of Cups
              </h3>
              <p>
                You will need to add an <code class=3D"systemitem">lp</code=
>
                user, as <span class=3D"application">Cups</span> will creat=
e
                some files owned by this user. (The <code class=3D"systemit=
em">lp</code> user is the default used by
                <span class=3D"application">Cups</span>, but may be changed=
 to
                a different user by passing a parameter to the <span class=
=3D"command"><strong>configure</strong></span> script.) Use the
                following command as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</c=
ode>
                user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">useradd -c "Print =
Service User" -d /var/spool/cups -g lp -s /bin/false -u 9 lp</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                You will also need a dedicated group that will contain user=
s
                allowed to do <span class=3D"application">Cups</span>
                administrative tasks. Add the group by running the followin=
g
                command as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">groupadd -g 19 lpa=
dmin</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                If you want to add a user to the <span class=3D"application=
">Cups</span> administrative group, run the
                following command as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</c=
ode>
                user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">usermod -a -G lpad=
min <em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;username&gt;</code></em></kbd></pre=
>
              <p>
                If you didn't install <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www=
.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.ht=
ml#xdg-utils" title=3D"xdg-utils-1.2.1">xdg-utils-1.2.1</a>, use the
                following <span class=3D"command"><strong>sed</strong></spa=
n>
                to change the default browser that will be used to access t=
he
                <span class=3D"application">Cups</span> web interface:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">sed -i 's#@CU=
PS_HTMLVIEW@#firefox#' desktop/cups.desktop.in</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                Replace <span class=3D"command"><strong>firefox</strong></s=
pan>
                with the web browser of your choice.
              </p>
              <p>
                Build <span class=3D"application">Cups</span> by running th=
e
                following commands:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">./configure -=
-libdir=3D/usr/lib            \
            --with-rcdir=3D/tmp/cupsinit   \
            --with-rundir=3D/run/cups      \
            --with-system-groups=3Dlpadmin \
            --with-docdir=3D/usr/share/cups/doc-2.4.10 &amp;&amp;
make</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                To test the results, issue: <span class=3D"command"><strong=
>LC_ALL=3DC make -k check</strong></span>. An
                already active graphical session with bus address is
                necessary to run the tests. Make sure that there is not oth=
er
                instance of <span class=3D"application">Cups</span> running=
,
                otherwise at least 4 tests will fail with "address in use".
              </p>
              <p>
                Now, as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">make install &amp;=
&amp;
ln -svnf ../cups/doc-2.4.10 /usr/share/doc/cups-2.4.10</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                Remove the shipped bootscript, which is incompatible with
                LFS:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">rm -rf /tmp/cupsin=
it</kbd></pre>
              <p>
                Create a basic <span class=3D"application">Cups</span> clie=
nt
                configuration file by running the following command as the
                <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
              </p>
              <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">echo "ServerName /=
run/cups/cups.sock" &gt; /etc/cups/client.conf</kbd></pre>
              <div class=3D"admon note">
                <h3>
                  Note
                </h3>
                <p>
                  This package installs icon files into the <code class=3D"=
filename">/usr/share/icons/hicolor</code> hierarchy and
                  you can improve system performance and memory usage by
                  updating <code class=3D"filename">/usr/share/icons/hicolo=
r/index.theme</code>. To
                  perform the update you must have <a class=3D"xref" href=
=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-s=
ysv-nochunks.html#gtk3" title=3D"GTK+-3.24.43">GTK+-3.24.43</a> installed a=
nd
                  issue the following command as the <code class=3D"systemi=
tem">root</code> user:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">gtk-update-=
icon-cache -qtf /usr/share/icons/hicolor</kbd></pre>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"commands" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Command Explanations
              </h3>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-rcdir=3D/tmp/cupsinit<=
/code></em>:
                This switch tells the build process to install the shipped
                bootscript into <code class=3D"filename">/tmp</code> instea=
d of
                <code class=3D"filename">/etc/rc.d</code>.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-rundir=3D/run/cups</co=
de></em>: This
                switch prevents the package from referring to the deprecate=
d
                <code class=3D"filename">/var/run</code> directory.
              </p>
              <p>
                <em class=3D"parameter"><code>--with-system-groups=3Dlpadmi=
n</code></em>:
                This switch ensures that only <code class=3D"systemitem">lp=
admin</code> will be used as the <span class=3D"application">Cups</span> ad=
ministrative group.
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">--disable-libusb</code>: Use this sw=
itch
                if you have installed <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www=
.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.ht=
ml#libusb" title=3D"libusb-1.0.27">libusb-1.0.27</a>, but wish to use the k=
ernel
                usblp driver.
              </p>
              <p>
                <code class=3D"option">--enable-libpaper</code>: Use this
                switch if you have installed <span class=3D"application">li=
bpaper</span> and wish to use it with
                <span class=3D"application">Cups</span>.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"configuration" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Configuring Cups
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"cups-config" name=3D"cups-config"></a>Conf=
ig
                        Files
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  <code class=3D"filename">/etc/cups/*</code>
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"idm140470477963648" name=3D"idm14047047796=
3648"></a>Configuration Information
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  Normally, printers are set up via a web browser. The
                  <span class=3D"application">Cups</span> server will norma=
lly
                  connect via the url http://localhost:631. From there
                  printers, print jobs, and the server configuration can be
                  set up and managed. Remote system administration can also
                  be set up. Configuration can also be done from the comman=
d
                  line via the <span class=3D"command"><strong>lpadmin</str=
ong></span>, <span class=3D"command"><strong>lpoptions</strong></span>, and
                  <span class=3D"command"><strong>lpstat</strong></span>
                  commands.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Configuration of <span class=3D"application">Cups</span> =
is
                  dependent on the type of printer and can be complex.
                  Generally, PostScript printers are easier. For detailed
                  instructions on configuration and use of <span class=3D"a=
pplication">Cups</span>, see <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://www.cups.or=
g/documentation.html">https://www.cups.org/documentation.html</a>.
                </p>
                <p>
                  For non-PostScript printers to print with <span class=3D"=
application">Cups</span>, you need to install <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"htt=
ps://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-noc=
hunks.html#gs" title=3D"ghostscript-10.03.1">ghostscript-10.03.1</a> to con=
vert
                  PostScript to raster images and a driver (e.g. from
                  <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https://www.linuxfromscratch.or=
g/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochunks.html#gutenprint" title=
=3D"Gutenprint-5.3.4">Gutenprint-5.3.4</a>) to convert the
                  resulting raster images to a form that the printer
                  understands. <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://wiki.linu=
xfoundation.org/openprinting/database/foomatic">
                  Foomatic</a> drivers use <a class=3D"xref" href=3D"https:=
//www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/downloads/stable/BLFS-BOOK-12.2-sysv-nochun=
ks.html#gs" title=3D"ghostscript-10.03.1">ghostscript-10.03.1</a> to conver=
t
                  PostScript to a printable form directly, but this is
                  considered suboptimal by <span class=3D"application">Cups=
</span> developers.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"idm140470477951760" name=3D"idm14047047795=
1760"></a>Linux PAM Configuration
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  If <span class=3D"application">CUPS</span> has been built
                  with <span class=3D"application">Linux PAM</span> support=
,
                  you need to create a <span class=3D"application">PAM</spa=
n>
                  configuration file to get it working correctly with BLFS.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Issue the following command as the <code class=3D"systemi=
tem">root</code> user to create the configuration
                  file for <span class=3D"application">Linux PAM</span>:
                </p>
