https://jai-tools.blogspot.com/">JAI Image I/O Tools</a>,
                and <a class=3D"ulink" href=3D"https://jeuclid.sourceforge.=
net/">JEuclid</a>
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"installation" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Installation of fop
              </h3>
              <p>
                Ensure <code class=3D"envar">$JAVA_HOME</code> is set corre=
ctly
                before beginning the build. To build the <span class=3D"app=
lication">JIMI SDK</span> and/or <span class=3D"application">XMLUnit</span>=
 extension classes, ensure the
                corresponding <code class=3D"filename">.jar</code> files ca=
n be
                found via the <code class=3D"envar">CLASSPATH</code>
                environment variable.
              </p>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"idm140470476063968" name=3D"idm14047047606=
3968"></a>Installing OFFO Hyphenation
                        Patterns
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  Copy the XML hyphenation patterns into the fop source tre=
e
                  by running the following commands:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">unzip ../of=
fo-hyphenation.zip &amp;&amp;
cp offo-hyphenation/hyph/* fop/hyph &amp;&amp;
rm -rf offo-hyphenation</kbd></pre>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"idm140470476061360" name=3D"idm14047047606=
1360"></a>Installing a temporary Maven
                        binary
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  Starting with fop-2.5, the Maven build system is required=
.
                  We use the binary provided by apache, that we install in =
a
                  temporary location:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">tar -xf ../=
apache-maven-3.9.4-bin.tar.gz -C /tmp</kbd></pre>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"idm140470476058864" name=3D"idm14047047605=
8864"></a>Installing fop Components
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  The <span class=3D"command"><strong>javadoc</strong></spa=
n>
                  command that ships with OpenJDK 10 and later has become
                  much stricter than previous versions regarding conformanc=
e
                  of the Javadoc comments in source code to HTML. The FOP
                  documentation does not meet those standards, so the
                  conformance checks have to be disabled. This can be done
                  with the following command:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">sed -i '\@&=
lt;/javad@i\
&lt;arg value=3D"-Xdoclint:none"/&gt;\
&lt;arg value=3D"--allow-script-in-comments"/&gt;\
&lt;arg value=3D"--ignore-source-errors"/&gt;' \
    fop/build.xml</kbd></pre>
                <p>
                  Compile <span class=3D"application">fop</span> by running=
 the
                  following commands:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">cd fop &amp=
;&amp;

LC_ALL=3Den_US.UTF-8                     \
PATH=3D$PATH:/tmp/apache-maven-3.9.4/bin \
ant all javadocs &amp;&amp;

mv build/javadocs .</kbd></pre>
                <p>
                  This package comes with a test suite, but the java
                  infrastructure installed in this book does not allow
                  running it.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Now, install <span class=3D"application">Fop</span> as th=
e
                  <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">install -v -d -m=
755 -o root -g root          /opt/fop-2.9 &amp;&amp;
cp -vR build conf examples fop* javadocs lib /opt/fop-2.9 &amp;&amp;
chmod a+x /opt/fop-2.9/fop                                &amp;&amp;
ln -v -sfn fop-2.9 /opt/fop</kbd></pre>
                <p>
                  The last thing to do is to clean what we have done:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">rm -rf /tmp=
/apache-maven-3.9.4</kbd></pre>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"commands" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Command Explanations
              </h3>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>sed -i ...
                build.xml</strong></span>: This adds three switches to the
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>javadoc</strong></span>
                command, preventing some errors from occurring when buildin=
g
                the documentation.
              </p>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>export
                LC_ALL=3Den_US.UTF-8</strong></span>: the compiler fails if
                using an ASCII locale.
              </p>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>ant <code class=3D"option">=
target</code></strong></span>: This reads the file
                <code class=3D"filename">build.xml</code> and builds the
                target: <code class=3D"option">compile</code> compiles the =
java
                sources, <code class=3D"option">jar-main</code> generates j=
ar
                archives, <code class=3D"option">jar-hyphenation</code>
                generates the hyphenation patterns for FOP, <code class=3D"=
option">junit</code> runs the <span class=3D"application">junit</span> test=
s, and <code class=3D"option">javadocs</code> builds the documentation. The
                <code class=3D"option">all</code> target runs all of the ab=
ove.
              </p>
              <p>
                <span class=3D"command"><strong>ln -v -sf fop-2.9
                /opt/fop</strong></span>: This is optional and creates a
                convenience symlink so that <code class=3D"envar">$FOP_HOME=
</code> doesn't have to be changed each time
                there's a package version change.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"configuration" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Configuring fop
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"fop-config" name=3D"fop-config"></a>Config=
 Files
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  <code class=3D"filename">~/.foprc</code>
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class=3D"sect3" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
                <div class=3D"titlepage">
                  <div>
                    <div>
                      <h4 class=3D"title">
                        <a id=3D"idm140470476029792" name=3D"idm14047047602=
9792"></a>Configuration Information
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p>
                  Using <span class=3D"application">fop</span> to process s=
ome
                  large FO's (including the FO derived from the BLFS XML
                  sources), can lead to memory errors. Unless you add a
                  parameter to the <span class=3D"command"><strong>java</st=
rong></span> command used in the
                  <span class=3D"command"><strong>fop</strong></span> scrip=
t
                  you may receive messages similar to the one shown below:
                </p>
                <p>
                  <code class=3D"computeroutput">Exception in thread "main"
                  java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space</code>
                </p>
                <p>
                  To avoid errors like this, you need to pass an extra
                  parameter to the <span class=3D"command"><strong>java</st=
rong></span> command used in the
                  <span class=3D"command"><strong>fop</strong></span> scrip=
t.
                  This can be accomplished by creating a <code class=3D"fil=
ename">~/.foprc</code> (which is sourced by the
                  <span class=3D"command"><strong>fop</strong></span> scrip=
t)
                  and adding the parameter to the <code class=3D"envar">FOP=
_OPTS</code> environment variable.
                </p>
                <p>
                  The <span class=3D"command"><strong>fop</strong></span>
                  script looks for a <code class=3D"envar">FOP_HOME</code>
                  environment variable to locate the <span class=3D"applica=
tion">fop</span> class libraries. You can create
                  this variable using the <code class=3D"filename">~/.foprc=
</code> file as well. Create a
                  <code class=3D"filename">~/.foprc</code> file using the
                  following commands:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"userinput"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt; ~/=
.foprc &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal">FOP_OPTS=3D"-Xmx<em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt=
;RAM_Installed&gt;</code></em>m"
FOP_HOME=3D"/opt/fop"</code>
EOF</kbd></pre>
                <p>
                  Replace <em class=3D"replaceable"><code>&lt;RAM_Installed=
&gt;</code></em> with
                  a number representing the amount of RAM installed in your
                  computer (in megabytes). An example would be <strong clas=
s=3D"userinput"><code>FOP_OPTS=3D"-Xmx768m"</code></strong>.
                </p>
                <p>
                  To include the <span class=3D"command"><strong>fop</stron=
g></span> script in your path,
                  update the system-wide profile with the following command
                  as the <code class=3D"systemitem">root</code> user:
                </p>
                <pre class=3D"root"><kbd class=3D"command">cat &gt; /etc/pr=
ofile.d/fop.sh &lt;&lt; "EOF"
<code class=3D"literal"># Begin /etc/profile.d/fop.sh

pathappend /opt/fop

# End /etc/profile.d/fop.sh</code>
EOF</kbd></pre>
                <div class=3D"admon note">
                  <h3>
                    Note
                  </h3>
                  <p>
                    Running <span class=3D"command"><strong>fop</strong></s=
pan>
                    can be somewhat verbose. The default logging level can =
be
                    changed from INFO to any of FINEST, FINER, FINE, CONFIG=
,
                    INFO, WARNING, SEVERE, ALL, or OFF. To do this, edit
                    <code class=3D"filename">$JAVA_HOME/jre/lib/logging.pro=
perties</code>
                    and change the entries for <code class=3D"option">.leve=
l</code> and <code class=3D"option">java.util.logging.ConsoleHandler.level<=
/code> to
                    the desired value.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class=3D"content" lang=3D"en" xml:lang=3D"en">
              <h3 class=3D"sect2">
                Contents
              </h3>
              <div class=3D"segmentedlist">
